 on a lie.\n *\n * It is called precision rather than specificity because that is what it is: of the population the\n * system declined to promote, the fraction it was right to decline. The denominator is the declines,\n * not the hypotheticals, so a system that declines nothing scores zero rather than undefined.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY NOBODY MEASURES IT\n *\n * Because nobody's extractor is allowed to decline. A rationale miner over commit messages has no\n * hypotheticals to decline ΓÇö a commit message does not speculate. Agent dialogue is the first corpus\n * in this literature where speculation, reported speech and rejected proposals outnumber decisions,\n * which is why the metric exists here and not in the surveyed systems. Reporting it as \"no comparator\"\n * is accurate rather than modest.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE DEMO-KILLER THIS GUARDS\n *\n * ┬º7.4 names \"hypotheticals becoming nodes\" as one of four demo-killers. This is the number that says\n * whether it happens. It is reported per transcript tier as well as in aggregate, because the\n * adversarial tier is where the failure lives and an aggregate over twelve transcripts can hide a\n * catastrophic 0.0 on three of them behind a good score on nine.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { EngineId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { Decision } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus, ResolvedDecision } from \"../corpus/labels\";\nimport type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport { overlapOf } from \"./match\";\nimport { METRIC_NAMES, describeEngineId, type MetricResult } from \"./types\";\n\nexport interface ModalityOutcome {\n  readonly metric: MetricResult;\n  /** Statements the corpus says must NOT become nodes, and did. Each one is a visible defect. */\n  readonly wronglyPromoted: readonly { label: ResolvedDecision; node: Decision }[];\n  /** Statements the corpus says must not become nodes, and did not. Correct declines. */\n  readonly correctlyDeclined: number;\n  readonly declinesExpected: number;\n  readonly byTier: readonly { tier: string; expected: number; wronglyPromoted: number }[];\n}\n\nexport function scoreModalityPrecision(corpus: ResolvedCorpus, run: RunResult, engine: EngineId): ModalityOutcome {\n  const nodes: Decision[] = [...run.state.decisions.values()];\n  const declines: { label: ResolvedDecision; tier: string }[] = [];\n\n  for (const transcript of corpus.transcripts) {\n    for (const label of transcript.decisions) {\n      if (label.expectNode) continue;\n      declines.push({ label, tier: transcript.tier });\n    }\n  }\n\n  const wronglyPromoted: { label: ResolvedDecision; node: Decision }[] = [];\n\n  for (const entry of declines) {\n    /* A decline is violated when ANY node overlaps the labelled span. Variable agreement is not\n     * required, because the failure mode is not \"found it and mislabelled the variable\" ΓÇö it is \"made\n     * a node out of a sentence that was not a decision\", and such a node may carry any variable. */\n    const hit = nodes.find((node) =>\n      overlapOf(entry.label.span, { start: node.provenance.snippet.start, end: node.provenance.snippet.end }) > 0,\n    );\n    if (hit !== undefined) wronglyPromoted.push({ label: entry.label, node: hit });\n  }\n\n  const correctlyDeclined = declines.length - wronglyPromoted.length;\n  const value = declines.length === 0 ? 1 : correctlyDeclined / declines.length;\n\n  const tiers = [...new Set(corpus.transcripts.map((transcript) => transcript.tier))];\n  const byTier = tiers.map((tier) => {\n    const expected = declines.filter((entry) => entry.tier === tier).length;\n    const wrong = wronglyPromoted.filter((entry) => tierOf(corpus, entry.label) === tier).length;\n    return { tier, expected, wronglyPromoted: wrong };\n  });\n\n  return {\n    wronglyPromoted,\n    correctlyDeclined,\n    declinesExpected: declines.length,\n    byTier,\n    metric: {\n      id: \"modality-precision\",\n      name: METRIC_NAMES[\"modality-precision\"],\n      value,\n      detail: null,\n      n: declines.length,\n      perEngine: [{ engine: describeEngineId(engine), value, n: declines.length, detail: null }],\n      comparator: null,\n      gloss:\n        correctlyDeclined + \" of \" + declines.length + \" non-decisions were correctly left out of the graph; \" +\n        wronglyPromoted.length + \" became nodes anyway.\",\n      honestNote:\n        \"This is a PRECISION over declines, not a recall over hypotheticals: the denominator is the statements the corpus \" +\n        \"marks as must-not-become-nodes, so a system that declines nothing scores 0 rather than undefined. n=\" +\n        declines.length + \", concentrated in the adversarial tier, which is small enough that one violation moves the number \" +\n        \"by several points ΓÇö the per-tier breakdown is printed beside it for that reason. No comparator exists because no \" +\n        \"surveyed extractor operates on a corpus where speculation outnumbers decisions; the absence is a property of the \" +\n        \"literature, not a gap in this measurement.\",\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nfunction tierOf(corpus: ResolvedCorpus, label: ResolvedDecision): string {\n  for (const transcript of corpus.transcripts) {\n    if (transcript.decisions.some((candidate) => candidate === label)) return transcript.tier;\n  }\n  return \"unknown\";\n}\n"},"tags":["fixable"],"source":null},{"category":"format","severity":"error","description":"Formatter would have printed the following content:","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Formatter would have printed the following content:"}],"advices":{"advices":[{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * Metric 5 ΓÇö premise-invalidation recall at L2 (non-monotonic).\n *       * decide, and counting indecision as detection would let a system pass by refusing to commit. */\n      const labelled = run.labels.get(id);\n      if (labelled !== undefined && (labelled.label === \"OUT\" || labelled.label === \"UNFOUNDED\"))\n        flipped.push(key);\n      else notFlipped.push(key);\n    }\n\n    return {plant: plant.id,testable,expectedUnsound: plant.expectUnsound,flipped,notFlipped,unresolvable,\n    };\n  });\n\n  const expected = perPlant.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.expectedUnsound.length, 0);\n  const hit = perPlant.filterperPlant\n    .filter((entry) => entry.testable).reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.flipped.length, 0);\n  const value = expected === 0 ? 0 : hit / expected;\n  }\n\n  const premiseGateRecords = run.state.contradictions.filter((record) => record.gate === \"premise\").length;\n\n  return {      n: expected,\n      perEngine: [{ engine: describeEngineId(engine), value, n: expected, detail: null }],\n      comparator: null /* no comparator exists; COMPARATORS records that this is the finding */\n      gloss:\n        hit +\" of \" +expected +\" decision(s) that should have gone unsound when their premise was falsified actually did. \" +\n        outListPopulated +\" justification(s) carry a non-empty OUT-list, which is what makes the graph non-monotonic at all. \" +\n        premiseGateRecords +\" premise-gate record(s) were raised.\",\n      honestNote:\n        \"NO PUBLISHED COMPARATOR EXISTS ΓÇö no surveyed system detects that a decision's premise became false without the \" +\n        \"decision being mentioned, so there is no component to compare against and the absence is reported rather than \" +\n        \"filled with a borrowed number. n=\" +\", which is ONE planted case (demo1's SQLite decision falsified by \" +\n        \"demo2). A single case supports the claim that the mechanism fires; it does not support any claim about a RATE, and \" +\n        \"no rate should be quoted from this metric. Measured with NO harness intervention: the refutation must be extracted \" +    },\n  };\n}","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,73]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":86}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[73,317]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[326,397]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,324]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[397,413]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,324]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[413,422]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,324]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[422,459]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,324]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[459,467]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,324]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[467,478]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,324]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[478,490]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[73,74]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[490,496]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[496,611]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[611,626]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[626,646]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[646,673]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,322]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[673,782]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":3}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[782,848]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,322]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[848,885]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[490,494]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[885,906]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":9}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[906,1038]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[490,491]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1038,1111]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[490,491]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1111,1138]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[1038,1039]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1138,1146]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[1038,1039]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1146,1156]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[906,907]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1156,1277]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[906,907]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1277,1408]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[906,907]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1408,1760]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[906,907]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1146,1156]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[906,907]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[317,326]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1760,2086]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":4}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[2086,2099]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[1111,1112]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1111,1112]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\metrics\\invalidation.ts"},"span":null,"sourceCode":"/**\n * Metric 5 ΓÇö premise-invalidation recall at L2 (non-monotonic).\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.2\n * Comparator: NONE PUBLISHED. No surveyed system has this component.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE HEADLINE RESULT, AND THE REASON THE PRODUCT EXISTS\n *\n * L1 fires when YOU contradict yourself. L2 fires when REALITY does: the decision is never mentioned,\n * its variable is never mentioned, no new statement conflicts with it ΓÇö and it is nonetheless no\n * longer true, because an assumption it rested on stopped holding.\n *\n * The planted case is the product's whole argument in one transcript. `demo2` says there are now four\n * people on the project and two of them edit decisions concurrently. It never says the word database.\n * The SQLite decision from `demo1` excluded concurrent writers, so it becomes unsound ΓÇö and the only\n * reason any system could notice is that it stored the exclusion.\n *\n * Storing the decision is what every competitor does. Storing the `given` is what none of them does,\n * and this metric measures whether storing it actually buys the detection.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * NO INTERVENTION: MEASURED THE HARD WAY ON PURPOSE\n *\n * Unlike the L3 plant, this one carries no harness-applied falsification. The refutation has to be\n * recovered from demo2's own text by extraction, and connected to demo1's premise by the reasoning\n * layer. That is two failure points instead of zero, and the number will be worse for it.\n *\n * It is measured this way because it is the claim being made. An L2 recall computed after the harness\n * hands the system the falsified premise measures the cascade, not the detection ΓÇö and the cascade is\n * already measured by metric 6. Reporting a hand-fed L2 number as evidence for \"the world moved and the\n * log noticed\" would be the one genuinely dishonest number available in this file.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE OUT-LIST IS WHAT MAKES THIS NON-MONOTONIC\n *\n * A Doyle SL justification is valid iff every IN-list node is IN AND no OUT-list node is IN. A\n * non-empty OUT-list is the formal signature of non-monotonic reasoning, and it is gate (c). A system\n * with only IN-lists is monotonic: adding information can never retract a conclusion, so it can never\n * notice that the world moved. This metric is therefore also a measurement of whether the OUT-list is\n * populated at all ΓÇö and `outListPopulated` is reported beside the recall for exactly that reason.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { EngineId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus } from \"../corpus/labels\";\nimport type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport { plantIsTestable, type Resolver } from \"./resolve\";\nimport { COMPARATORS, METRIC_NAMES, describeEngineId, type MetricResult } from \"./types\";\n\nexport interface InvalidationOutcome {\n  readonly metric: MetricResult;\n  readonly perPlant: readonly {\n    plant: string;\n    testable: boolean;\n    expectedUnsound: readonly string[];\n    flipped: readonly string[];\n    notFlipped: readonly string[];\n    unresolvable: readonly string[];\n  }[];\n  /** How many decisions in the graph carry a non-empty OUT-list. Zero means the graph is monotonic and\n   *  this metric cannot score above zero for a reason that has nothing to do with detection quality. */\n  readonly outListPopulated: number;\n  readonly decisionsTotal: number;\n  readonly premiseGateRecords: number;\n}\n\nexport function scoreInvalidationRecall(\n  corpus: ResolvedCorpus,\n  run: RunResult,\n  resolver: Resolver,\n  engine: EngineId,\n): InvalidationOutcome {\n  const plants = corpus.plants.filter((plant) => plant.kind === \"L2-premise\");\n\n  const perPlant = plants.map((plant) => {\n    const testable = plantIsTestable(run, plant.transcripts);\n    const flipped: string[] = [];\n    const notFlipped: string[] = [];\n    const unresolvable: string[] = [];\n\n    for (const key of plant.expectUnsound) {\n      const id = resolver.decision(key);\n      if (id === null) {\n        unresolvable.push(key);\n        continue;\n      }\n\n      /* \"Unsound\" is label OUT or UNFOUNDED. UNKNOWN is not a flip: it means the labeller could not\n       * decide, and counting indecision as detection would let a system pass by refusing to commit. */\n      const labelled = run.labels.get(id);\n      if (labelled !== undefined && (labelled.label === \"OUT\" || labelled.label === \"UNFOUNDED\")) flipped.push(key);\n      else notFlipped.push(key);\n    }\n\n    return { plant: plant.id, testable, expectedUnsound: plant.expectUnsound, flipped, notFlipped, unresolvable };\n  });\n\n  const expected = perPlant.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.expectedUnsound.length, 0);\n  const hit = perPlant.filter((entry) => entry.testable).reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.flipped.length, 0);\n  const value = expected === 0 ? 0 : hit / expected;\n\n  let outListPopulated = 0;\n  for (const justification of run.state.justifications.values()) {\n    if (justification.outList.length > 0) outListPopulated += 1;\n  }\n\n  const premiseGateRecords = run.state.contradictions.filter((record) => record.gate === \"premise\").length;\n\n  return {\n    perPlant,\n    outListPopulated,\n    decisionsTotal: run.state.decisions.size,\n    premiseGateRecords,\n    metric: {\n      id: \"invalidation-recall-l2\",\n      name: METRIC_NAMES[\"invalidation-recall-l2\"],\n      value,\n      detail: null,\n      n: expected,\n      perEngine: [{ engine: describeEngineId(engine), value, n: expected, detail: null }],\n      comparator: null, /* no comparator exists; COMPARATORS records that this is the finding */\n      gloss:\n        hit + \" of \" + expected + \" decision(s) that should have gone unsound when their premise was falsified actually did. \" +\n        outListPopulated + \" justification(s) carry a non-empty OUT-list, which is what makes the graph non-monotonic at all. \" +\n        premiseGateRecords + \" premise-gate record(s) were raised.\",\n      honestNote:\n        \"NO PUBLISHED COMPARATOR EXISTS ΓÇö no surveyed system detects that a decision's premise became false without the \" +\n        \"decision being mentioned, so there is no component to compare against and the absence is reported rather than \" +\n        \"filled with a borrowed number. n=\" + expected + \", which is ONE planted case (demo1's SQLite decision falsified by \" +\n        \"demo2). A single case supports the claim that the mechanism fires; it does not support any claim about a RATE, and \" +\n        \"no rate should be quoted from this metric. Measured with NO harness intervention: the refutation must be extracted \" +\n        \"from demo2's text and connected to demo1's premise by the reasoning layer, so a miss here may be an extraction \" +\n        \"failure rather than a reasoning one ΓÇö check premise-extraction before blaming gate (c). If outListPopulated is 0 \" +\n        \"the graph is monotonic and this metric is structurally incapable of scoring, which is a different finding from a \" +\n        \"detection failure and is printed beside the number for that reason.\",\n    },\n  };\n}\n\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"organizeImports","severity":"error","description":"Import statements could be sorted:","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Import statements could be sorted:"}],"advices":{"advices":[{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * Metric 13 ΓÇö modality precision: of the statements the system DECLINED, how many were correctly declined.\n *import type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport { overlapOf } from \"./match\";\nimport { METRIC_NAMES, describeEngineId, type MetricResult, describeEngineId } from \"./types\";\n\nexport interface ModalityOutcome {  return \"unknown\";\n}\n","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,116]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":37}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[116,221]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[221,239]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[239,256]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[256,274]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[274,328]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":73}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[328,350]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\metrics\\modality.ts"},"span":null,"sourceCode":"/**\n * Metric 13 ΓÇö modality precision: of the statements the system DECLINED, how many were correctly declined.\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.2\n * Comparator: none published. ┬º7.2 calls this one of the two unusual metrics.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE INVERSE OF THE USUAL QUESTION, AND THE ONE THAT MATTERS HERE\n *\n * Every extraction metric asks \"did you find the things that are there\". This one asks \"did you leave\n * alone the things that are not decisions\". A system that turns \"what if we used Redis?\" into a Redis\n * decision has perfect recall and is unusable, because the graph now contains things nobody decided\n * and every downstream guarantee ΓÇö contradiction detection, cascade, export ΓÇö is built on a lie.\n *\n * It is called precision rather than specificity because that is what it is: of the population the\n * system declined to promote, the fraction it was right to decline. The denominator is the declines,\n * not the hypotheticals, so a system that declines nothing scores zero rather than undefined.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY NOBODY MEASURES IT\n *\n * Because nobody's extractor is allowed to decline. A rationale miner over commit messages has no\n * hypotheticals to decline ΓÇö a commit message does not speculate. Agent dialogue is the first corpus\n * in this literature where speculation, reported speech and rejected proposals outnumber decisions,\n * which is why the metric exists here and not in the surveyed systems. Reporting it as \"no comparator\"\n * is accurate rather than modest.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE DEMO-KILLER THIS GUARDS\n *\n * ┬º7.4 names \"hypotheticals becoming nodes\" as one of four demo-killers. This is the number that says\n * whether it happens. It is reported per transcript tier as well as in aggregate, because the\n * adversarial tier is where the failure lives and an aggregate over twelve transcripts can hide a\n * catastrophic 0.0 on three of them behind a good score on nine.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { EngineId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { Decision } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus, ResolvedDecision } from \"../corpus/labels\";\nimport type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport { overlapOf } from \"./match\";\nimport { METRIC_NAMES, describeEngineId, type MetricResult } from \"./types\";\n\nexport interface ModalityOutcome {\n  readonly metric: MetricResult;\n  /** Statements the corpus says must NOT become nodes, and did. Each one is a visible defect. */\n  readonly wronglyPromoted: readonly { label: ResolvedDecision; node: Decision }[];\n  /** Statements the corpus says must not become nodes, and did not. Correct declines. */\n  readonly correctlyDeclined: number;\n  readonly declinesExpected: number;\n  readonly byTier: readonly { tier: string; expected: number; wronglyPromoted: number }[];\n}\n\nexport function scoreModalityPrecision(corpus: ResolvedCorpus, run: RunResult, engine: EngineId): ModalityOutcome {\n  const nodes: Decision[] = [...run.state.decisions.values()];\n  const declines: { label: ResolvedDecision; tier: string }[] = [];\n\n  for (const transcript of corpus.transcripts) {\n    for (const label of transcript.decisions) {\n      if (label.expectNode) continue;\n      declines.push({ label, tier: transcript.tier });\n    }\n  }\n\n  const wronglyPromoted: { label: ResolvedDecision; node: Decision }[] = [];\n\n  for (const entry of declines) {\n    /* A decline is violated when ANY node overlaps the labelled span. Variable agreement is not\n     * required, because the failure mode is not \"found it and mislabelled the variable\" ΓÇö it is \"made\n     * a node out of a sentence that was not a decision\", and such a node may carry any variable. */\n    const hit = nodes.find((node) =>\n      overlapOf(entry.label.span, { start: node.provenance.snippet.start, end: node.provenance.snippet.end }) > 0,\n    );\n    if (hit !== undefined) wronglyPromoted.push({ label: entry.label, node: hit });\n  }\n\n  const correctlyDeclined = declines.length - wronglyPromoted.length;\n  const value = declines.length === 0 ? 1 : correctlyDeclined / declines.length;\n\n  const tiers = [...new Set(corpus.transcripts.map((transcript) => transcript.tier))];\n  const byTier = tiers.map((tier) => {\n    const expected = declines.filter((entry) => entry.tier === tier).length;\n    const wrong = wronglyPromoted.filter((entry) => tierOf(corpus, entry.label) === tier).length;\n    return { tier, expected, wronglyPromoted: wrong };\n  });\n\n  return {\n    wronglyPromoted,\n    correctlyDeclined,\n    declinesExpected: declines.length,\n    byTier,\n    metric: {\n      id: \"modality-precision\",\n      name: METRIC_NAMES[\"modality-precision\"],\n      value,\n      detail: null,\n      n: declines.length,\n      perEngine: [{ engine: describeEngineId(engine), value, n: declines.length, detail: null }],\n      comparator: null,\n      gloss:\n        correctlyDeclined + \" of \" + declines.length + \" non-decisions were correctly left out of the graph; \" +\n        wronglyPromoted.length + \" became nodes anyway.\",\n      honestNote:\n        \"This is a PRECISION over declines, not a recall over hypotheticals: the denominator is the statements the corpus \" +\n        \"marks as must-not-become-nodes, so a system that declines nothing scores 0 rather than undefined. n=\" +\n        declines.length + \", concentrated in the adversarial tier, which is small enough that one violation moves the number \" +\n        \"by several points ΓÇö the per-tier breakdown is printed beside it for that reason. No comparator exists because no \" +\n        \"surveyed extractor operates on a corpus where speculation outnumbers decisions; the absence is a property of the \" +\n        \"literature, not a gap in this measurement.\",\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nfunction tierOf(corpus: ResolvedCorpus, label: ResolvedDecision): string {\n  for (const transcript of corpus.transcripts) {\n    if (transcript.decisions.some((candidate) => candidate === label)) return transcript.tier;\n  }\n  return \"unknown\";\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"format","severity":"error","description":"Formatter would have printed the following content:","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Formatter would have printed the following content:"}],"advices":{"advices":[{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * Metric 12 ΓÇö guard precision on seeded violations.\n *}\n\nexport function scoreGuards(\n  corpus: ResolvedCorpus, run: RunResult,engine: EngineId,\n): GuardOutcome {\n  const guards: Guard[] = [...run.state.guards.values()];\n  const decisionsTotal = run.state.decisions.size;    if (decision === undefined) return false;\n    const shouldBeDisabled =\n      decision.label === \"OUT\" ||\n      decision.label === \"UNFOUNDED\" ||decision.status === \"superseded\";\n    return shouldBeDisabled !== guard.disabledByDecisionLabel;\n  }).length;      gloss:\n        \"NOT MEASURED ΓÇö n=0. This corpus is twelve transcripts with no repository state, so no violation could be seeded \" +\n        \"and no guard had anything to fail against. Measured instead: synthesis rate \" +\n        (synthesisRate * 100).toFixed(0) +\n        \"% (\" +decisionsWithGuard +\" of \" +decisionsTotal +\" decisions got a proposed guard), determinism share \" +\n        (determinismShare * 100).toFixed(0) +\"% (\" +deterministic +\" deterministic, \" +judged +\" llm_judge), \" +\n        skippedCountedAsPass +\" skip(s) counted as a pass, \" +autoAccepted +\" guard(s) auto-accepted without a human, \" +\n        staleDisabled +\" guard(s) whose disabled flag disagrees with their decision's label.\",\n      honestNote:\n        \"NOT MEASURED, AND THIS IS A LIMITATION OF THE BENCHMARK RATHER THAN A RESULT ABOUT THE SYSTEM. Guard precision \" +\nexport type { ResolvedCorpus };\n","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,61]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":57}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[61,92]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[92,95]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[95,118]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[92,95]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[119,134]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[92,95]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[134,150]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[150,152]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[152,278]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":30}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[278,386]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[278,279]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[386,393]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[393,426]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[278,279]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[386,393]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[426,535]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":26}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[535,763]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[535,536]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[772,822]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[535,536]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[822,842]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[535,536]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[842,850]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[535,536]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[850,866]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[535,536]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[866,968]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[278,279]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[968,975]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[278,279]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[975,990]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[278,279]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[990,1010]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[278,279]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1010,1018]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1018,1066]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1066,1098]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1098,1112]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1112,1181]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[118,119]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[763,772]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1181,1394]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":14}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[1394,1427]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\metrics\\guards.ts"},"span":null,"sourceCode":"/**\n * Metric 12 ΓÇö guard precision on seeded violations.\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.2\n * Comparator: the fitness-function literature, qualitative throughout.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THIS METRIC IS NOT MEASURED, AND SAYING SO IS THE RESULT\n *\n * Guard precision needs seeded violations: a repository state that breaks a decision, with a label\n * saying which guard should catch it. This corpus is twelve TRANSCRIPTS. It contains no package.json,\n * no repository state and no violations, so there is nothing for a guard to fail against and no\n * denominator for a precision.\n *\n * The honest output is `n = 0` and a note explaining what would be needed, not a proxy number dressed\n * up as precision. A benchmark that reports 1.0 for guard precision because no guard ever ran is\n * reporting that it measured nothing, in a format designed to look like success.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHAT IS MEASURED INSTEAD, AND LABELLED AS SUCH\n *\n * Four properties of the guard layer that a transcript corpus CAN test, reported as their own numbers\n * and never as precision:\n *\n *   1. SYNTHESIS RATE ΓÇö how many decisions got at least one proposed guard. This is the claim that\n *      matters most (nobody generates the check from the decision) and it needs no violations.\n *   2. DETERMINISM SHARE ΓÇö how many synthesised guards are deterministic rather than `llm_judge`.\n *      `llm_judge` is structurally excluded from compliance in Phase 4, so this is the fraction of the\n *      layer that can actually gate a CI run.\n *   3. SKIP DISCIPLINE ΓÇö whether any skipped guard was counted as a pass. Phase 4 settled that skips\n *      are never passes; this checks the settlement held.\n *   4. AUTO-INSTALL ΓÇö whether any guard reached `accepted` without a human. Invariant I4 says the\n *      system proposes and the human disposes, and a harness that never acts as a human should find\n *      zero accepted guards. Finding one would be a real defect.\n *\n * All four are structural checks on the corpus this benchmark actually has, and all four would still be\n * worth reporting even with a perfect violation-seeded precision beside them.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { EngineId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { Guard } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus } from \"../corpus/labels\";\nimport type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport { COMPARATORS, METRIC_NAMES, describeEngineId, type MetricResult } from \"./types\";\n\nexport interface GuardOutcome {\n  readonly metric: MetricResult;\n  readonly guards: number;\n  readonly decisionsWithGuard: number;\n  readonly decisionsTotal: number;\n  readonly synthesisRate: number;\n  readonly deterministic: number;\n  readonly judged: number;\n  readonly determinismShare: number;\n  readonly skippedCountedAsPass: number;\n  readonly autoAccepted: number;\n  /** Guards whose `disabledByDecisionLabel` disagrees with their decision's actual label. Must be 0. */\n  readonly staleDisabledFlags: number;\n  readonly byKind: Readonly<Record<string, number>>;\n}\n\nexport function scoreGuards(corpus: ResolvedCorpus, run: RunResult, engine: EngineId): GuardOutcome {\n  const guards: Guard[] = [...run.state.guards.values()];\n  const decisionsTotal = run.state.decisions.size;\n\n  const decisionsWithGuard = new Set(guards.map((guard) => guard.decisionId)).size;\n  const synthesisRate = decisionsTotal === 0 ? 0 : decisionsWithGuard / decisionsTotal;\n\n  /* `llm_judge` is the only non-deterministic kind, and Phase 4 excludes it from compliance by\n   * filtering on `isDeterministic` with no code path around the filter. Counting it here checks that\n   * the exclusion has something to exclude ΓÇö a determinism share of 1.0 with zero guards is vacuous. */\n  const deterministic = guards.filter((guard) => guard.spec.kind !== \"llm_judge\").length;\n  const judged = guards.length - deterministic;\n  const determinismShare = guards.length === 0 ? 0 : deterministic / guards.length;\n\n  const byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const guard of guards) {\n    const kind: string = guard.spec.kind;\n    byKind[kind] = (byKind[kind] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n\n  /* A skip counted as a pass would be invisible in the compliance metric and fatal in CI: the pipeline\n   * goes green because the check never ran. Detected by looking for guards whose last run skipped but\n   * whose lifecycle reads as satisfied. */\n  const skippedCountedAsPass = guards.filter(\n    (guard) => guard.lastRun?.outcome === \"skip\" && guard.lifecycle === \"accepted\",\n  ).length;\n\n  /* A guard whose decision went OUT or SUPERSEDED must be auto-disabled, or a reversed decision keeps\n   * nagging the user about a rule they no longer hold. Phase 6 found this field going stale in the fold\n   * and fixed it there; counting it here means a regression surfaces as a number in a table rather than\n   * as a bug noticed during the demo. */\n  const staleDisabled = guards.filter((guard) => {\n    const decision = run.state.decisions.get(guard.decisionId);\n    if (decision === undefined) return false;\n    const shouldBeDisabled =\n      decision.label === \"OUT\" || decision.label === \"UNFOUNDED\" || decision.status === \"superseded\";\n    return shouldBeDisabled !== guard.disabledByDecisionLabel;\n  }).length;\n\n  /* Invariant I4. The harness never acts as a human, so every accepted guard is one the system\n   * accepted for itself. */\n  const autoAccepted = guards.filter((guard) => guard.lifecycle === \"accepted\").length;\n\n  return {\n    guards: guards.length,\n    decisionsWithGuard,\n    decisionsTotal,\n    synthesisRate,\n    deterministic,\n    judged,\n    determinismShare,\n    skippedCountedAsPass,\n    autoAccepted,\n    staleDisabledFlags: staleDisabled,\n    byKind,\n    metric: {\n      id: \"guard-precision\",\n      name: METRIC_NAMES[\"guard-precision\"],\n      /* Zero, not a proxy. The value is meaningless without n and the gloss says so first. */\n      value: 0,\n      detail: null,\n      n: 0,\n      perEngine: [{ engine: describeEngineId(engine), value: 0, n: 0, detail: null }],\n      comparator: COMPARATORS[\"guard-precision\"] ?? null,\n      gloss:\n        \"NOT MEASURED ΓÇö n=0. This corpus is twelve transcripts with no repository state, so no violation could be seeded \" +\n        \"and no guard had anything to fail against. Measured instead: synthesis rate \" + (synthesisRate * 100).toFixed(0) +\n        \"% (\" + decisionsWithGuard + \" of \" + decisionsTotal + \" decisions got a proposed guard), determinism share \" +\n        (determinismShare * 100).toFixed(0) + \"% (\" + deterministic + \" deterministic, \" + judged + \" llm_judge), \" +\n        skippedCountedAsPass + \" skip(s) counted as a pass, \" + autoAccepted + \" guard(s) auto-accepted without a human, \" +\n        staleDisabled + \" guard(s) whose disabled flag disagrees with their decision's label.\",\n      honestNote:\n        \"NOT MEASURED, AND THIS IS A LIMITATION OF THE BENCHMARK RATHER THAN A RESULT ABOUT THE SYSTEM. Guard precision \" +\n        \"requires seeded violations ΓÇö a repository state that breaks a decision, labelled with which guard should catch it \" +\n        \"ΓÇö and a transcript corpus contains no repository state. Reporting a proxy as precision, or reporting 1.0 because \" +\n        \"no guard ever failed, would both be the same error in different clothes: a number in a format designed to look \" +\n        \"like a measurement. The four structural properties that ARE testable on this corpus are reported in the gloss and \" +\n        \"are worth having on their own ΓÇö synthesis rate is the central novelty claim (no surveyed tool generates the check \" +\n        \"from the decision) and needs no violations at all. Three of the reported counts are INVARIANT CHECKS rather than \" +\n        \"measurements and must be zero: skips counted as passes, guards accepted without a human (invariant I4 ΓÇö the harness \" +\n        \"never acts as a human, so any accepted guard is one the system accepted for itself), and guards whose disabled flag \" +\n        \"disagrees with their decision's label. A non-zero value in any of them is a defect in Phase 4 or in the fold, not a \" +\n        \"property of the corpus. Seeding real violations needs a fixture repository, which is roadmap work and is recorded \" +\n        \"in LIMITATIONS.\",\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nexport type { ResolvedCorpus };\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/suspicious/noExplicitAny","severity":"error","description":"Unexpected any. Specify a different type.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Unexpected "},{"elements":["Emphasis"],"content":"any"},{"elements":[],"content":". Specify a different type."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":["Emphasis"],"content":"any"},{"elements":[],"content":" disables many type checking rules. Its use should be avoided."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\metrics\\resolve.ts"},"span":[1830,1833],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Label keys to runtime ids.\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.2\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY THIS EXISTS\n *\n * The corpus names nodes `\"l03/d1\"` ΓÇö a stable, readable key. The graph names them `WLD-0007`, minted\n * from a counter whose value depends on how many nodes every earlier transcript produced. Neither can\n * be written in terms of the other: hardcoding runtime ids in the corpus would make the labels break\n * whenever extraction improves, and deriving corpus keys from the graph would make the ground truth a\n * function of the system being measured, which is circular.\n *\n * So the bridge is built at scoring time from the ALIGNMENT the extraction metrics already computed.\n * A label that did not align has no runtime id, and every metric that depends on it reports the plant\n * as UNTESTED rather than as passed. That distinction is the whole reason this module exists: a plant\n * whose decision was never extracted cannot have its cascade verified, and scoring it as a pass would\n * be the single most misleading number this benchmark could produce.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { AlternativeId, DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus } from \"../corpus/labels\";\nimport type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport type { Alignment } from \"./match\";\n\nexport interface Resolver {\n  decision(key: string): DecisionId | null;\n  premise(key: string): PremiseId | null;\n  alternative(key: string): AlternativeId | null;\n  /** Keys the corpus asked about that have no runtime node. Reported, never swallowed. */\n  readonly unresolvable: readonly string[];\n}\n\nexport interface Alignments<TDecision, TPremise, TAlternative> {\n  readonly decisions: Alignment<any, TDecision>;\n  readonly premises: Alignment<any, TPremise>;\n  readonly alternatives: Alignment<any, TAlternative>;\n}\n\nexport function buildResolver(\n  corpus: ResolvedCorpus,\n  run: RunResult,\n  decisions: Alignment<{ transcript: string; key: string }, { id: DecisionId }>,\n  premises: Alignment<{ transcript: string; key: string }, { id: PremiseId }>,\n  alternatives: Alignment<{ transcript: string; key: string }, { id: AlternativeId }>,\n): Resolver {\n  const decisionMap = new Map<string, DecisionId>();\n  const premiseMap = new Map<string, PremiseId>();\n  const alternativeMap = new Map<string, AlternativeId>();\n  const unresolvable: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const pair of decisions.pairs) decisionMap.set(pair.label.transcript + \"/\" + pair.label.key, pair.node.id);\n  for (const pair of premises.pairs) premiseMap.set(pair.label.transcript + \"/\" + pair.label.key, pair.node.id);\n  for (const pair of alternatives.pairs) alternativeMap.set(pair.label.transcript + \"/\" + pair.label.key, pair.node.id);\n\n  /* Every key the plants reference is checked up front, so a missing node is reported once, here,\n   * rather than surfacing as a mysterious zero in whichever metric happened to ask. */\n  for (const plant of corpus.plants) {\n    for (const key of [...plant.expectUnsound, ...plant.expectSurvivors]) {\n      if (!decisionMap.has(key) && !premiseMap.has(key)) unresolvable.push(plant.id + \" -> \" + key);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return {\n    decision: (key) => decisionMap.get(key) ?? null,\n    premise: (key) => premiseMap.get(key) ?? null,\n    alternative: (key) => alternativeMap.get(key) ?? null,\n    unresolvable,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Runtime id of the transcript with this corpus key, or null if it failed to import. */\nexport function runtimeTranscriptId(run: RunResult, corpusKey: string): string | null {\n  const entry = run.transcripts.find((candidate) => candidate.transcript.id === corpusKey);\n  return entry === undefined ? null : entry.runtimeId;\n}\n\n/** Whether a plant's transcripts all imported. A plant spanning a failed transcript is untested. */\nexport function plantIsTestable(run: RunResult, transcripts: readonly string[]): boolean {\n  return transcripts.every((key) => runtimeTranscriptId(run, key) !== null);\n}\n\nexport type { ResolvedCorpus };\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/suspicious/noExplicitAny","severity":"error","description":"Unexpected any. Specify a different type.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Unexpected "},{"elements":["Emphasis"],"content":"any"},{"elements":[],"content":". Specify a different type."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":["Emphasis"],"content":"any"},{"elements":[],"content":" disables many type checking rules. Its use should be avoided."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\metrics\\resolve.ts"},"span":[1878,1881],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Label keys to runtime ids.\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.2\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY THIS EXISTS\n *\n * The corpus names nodes `\"l03/d1\"` ΓÇö a stable, readable key. The graph names them `WLD-0007`, minted\n * from a counter whose value depends on how many nodes every earlier transcript produced. Neither can\n * be written in terms of the other: hardcoding runtime ids in the corpus would make the labels break\n * whenever extraction improves, and deriving corpus keys from the graph would make the ground truth a\n * function of the system being measured, which is circular.\n *\n * So the bridge is built at scoring time from the ALIGNMENT the extraction metrics already computed.\n * A label that did not align has no runtime id, and every metric that depends on it reports the plant\n * as UNTESTED rather than as passed. That distinction is the whole reason this module exists: a plant\n * whose decision was never extracted cannot have its cascade verified, and scoring it as a pass would\n * be the single most misleading number this benchmark could produce.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { AlternativeId, DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus } from \"../corpus/labels\";\nimport type { RunResult } from \"../harness\";\nimport type { Alignment } from \"./match\";\n\nexport interface Resolver {\n  decision(key: string): DecisionId | null;\n  premise(key: string): PremiseId | null;\n  alternative(key: string): AlternativeId | null;\n  /** Keys the corpus asked about that have no runtime node. Reported, never swallowed. */\n  readonly unresolvable: readonly string[];\n}\n\nexport interface Alignments<TDecision, TPremise, TAlternative> {\n  readonly decisions: Alignment<any, TDecision>;\n  readonly premises: Alignment<any, TPremise>;\n  readonly alternatives: Alignment<any, TAlternative>;\n}\n\nexport function buildResolver(\n  corpus: ResolvedCorpus,\n  run: RunResult,\n  decisions: Alignment<{ transcript: string; key: string }, { id: DecisionId }>,\n  premises: Alignment<{ transcript: string; key: string }, { id: PremiseId }>,\n  alternatives: Alignment<{ transcript: string; key: string }, { id: AlternativeId }>,\n): Resolver {\n  const decisionMap = new Map<string, DecisionId>();\n  const premiseMap = new Map<string, PremiseId>();\n  const alternativeMap = new Map<string, AlternativeId>();\n  const unresolvable: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const pair of decisions.pairs) decisionMap.set(pair.label.transcript + \"/\" + pair.label.key, pair.node.id);\n  for (const pair of premises.pairs) premiseMap.set(pair.label.transcript + \"/\" + pair.label.key, pair.node.id);\n  for (const pair of alternatives.pairs) alternativeMap.set(pair.label.transcript + \"/\" + pair.label.key, pair.node.id);\n\n  /* Every key the plants reference is checked up front, so a missing node is reported once, here,\n   * rather than surfacing as a mysterious zero in whichever metric happened to ask. */\n  for (const plant of corpus.plants) {\n    for (const key of [...plant.expectUnsound, ...plant.expectSurvivors]) {\n      if (!decisionMap.has(key) && !premiseMap.has(key)) unresolvable.push(plant.id + \" -> \" + key);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return {\n    decision: (key) => decisionMap.get(key) ?? null,\n    premise: (key) => premiseMap.get(key) ?? null,\n    alternative: (key) => alternativeMap.get(key) ?? null,\n    unresolvable,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Runtime id of the transcript with this corpus key, or null if it failed to import. */\nexport function runtimeTranscriptId(run: RunResult, corpusKey: string): string | null {\n  const entry = run.transcripts.find((candidate) => candidate.transcript.id === corpusKey);\n  return entry === undefined ? null : entry.runtimeId;\n}\n\n/** Whether a plant's transcripts all imported. A plant spanning a failed transcript is untested. */\nexport function plantIsTestable(run: RunResult, transcripts: readonly string[]): boolean {\n  return transcripts.every((key) => runtimeTranscriptId(run, key) !== null);\n}\n\nexport type { ResolvedCorpus };\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/suspicious/noExplicitAny","severity":"error","description":"Unexpected any. Specify a different type.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Unexpected "},{"elements":["Emphasis"],"content":"any"},{"elements":[],"content":". Specify a different type."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":["Emphasis"],"content":"any"},{"elements":[],"content":" disables many type checking rules. Its use should be avoided."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\metrics\\resolve.ts"},"span":[1929,1932],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Label keys to runtime ids.\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.2\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY THIS EXISTS\n *\n * The corpus names nodes `\"l03/d1\"` ΓÇö a stable, readable key. The graph names them `WLD-0007`, minted\n * from a counter whose value depends on how many nodes every earlier transcript produced. Neither can\n * be written in terms of the other: hardcoding runtime ids in the corpus would make the labels break\n * whenever extraction improves, and deriving corpus keys from the graph would make the ground truth a\n * function of the