:{"path":{"file":".\\src\\eval\\report.ts"},"span":null,"sourceCode":"/**\n * `npm run eval:report` ΓÇö write the full evaluation report to disk.\n *\n * Phase 7 ┬╖ sub-phase 7.7\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE REPORT SHOWS ITS WORKING\n *\n * Every headline number is followed by the per-plant and per-question breakdown that produced it, the\n * sample size, the comparator (or an explicit statement that none exists) and what the number does not\n * establish. A report of summary numbers alone would be indistinguishable from marketing, and G6 asks\n * that every claim be backed by a number or a diagram rather than an adjective ΓÇö which means the number\n * has to be traceable to the cases behind it.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * IT IS GENERATED, NOT WRITTEN\n *\n * `docs/EVALUATION.md` quotes from this file; it does not restate it. Two copies of a number drift, and\n * a benchmark whose published figures disagree with its own output is worse than one with no published\n * figures. Regenerating the report is the only way to keep them identical.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { describeCorpus } from \"./corpus/labels\";\nimport { describeLoad } from \"./corpus/loader\";\nimport type { ResolvedCorpus } from \"./corpus/labels\";\nimport type { ScoredRun } from \"./metrics\";\nimport { metricsWithoutComparator, renderSuite, unusualMetrics } from \"./metrics\";\nimport type { MetricResult } from \"./metrics/types\";\nimport { describeStudy, runAblation, type AblationStudy } from \"./ablation/run\";\nimport { harden, renderHardening, type HardeningReport } from \"./hardening\";\nimport { renderAblationTable, renderPredictionAudit } from \"./ablation/table\";\nimport { banner, blank, heading, line, prepare, readArgs, write } from \"./cli\";\nimport { runConfig } from \"./harness\";\nimport { scoreAll } from \"./metrics\";\nimport { DEFAULT_PARAMS, FULL } from \"./ablation/toggles\";\n\n/**\n * The whole report as Markdown.\n *\n * `study` is optional: the baseline report is useful without fifteen ablation runs, and requiring the\n * study would mean the fastest possible verification path ΓÇö load the corpus, run it once, print the\n * numbers ΓÇö took fifteen times longer than it needs to.\n */\nexport function renderFullReport(\n  corpus: ResolvedCorpus,\n  scored: ScoredRun,\n  study: AblationStudy | null,\n  hardening: HardeningReport | null = null,\n): string {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n\n  out.push(\"# DecisionMemEval ΓÇö evaluation report\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"Generated \" + nowISO() + \". Do not edit by hand: `npm run eval:report` regenerates it.\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"Corpus \" + corpus.version + \" ┬╖ engine \" + describeEngine(scored) + \" ┬╖ \" + corpus.transcripts.length + \" transcripts\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  /* ---- the honest summary first ---- */\n  out.push(\"## Summary\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(metricsSummary(scored));\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  /* ---- the suite ---- */\n  out.push(\"## The thirteen metrics\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"```\");\n  out.push(renderSuite(scored));\n  out.push(\"```\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  out.push(\"### Each metric, with what it does not establish\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  for (const metric of scored.metrics) out.push(...metricSection(metric));\n\n  /* ---- the two unusual ones, pulled forward ---- */\n  out.push(\"## The two metrics no competitor can produce\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\n    \"┬º7.2 calls these out specifically. Neither measures a capability the surveyed systems have at any\",\n    \"quality, because neither component exists in them: no surveyed system cascades a retraction, and no\",\n    \"surveyed extractor operates on a corpus where speculation outnumbers decisions.\",\n  );\n  out.push(\"\");\n  for (const metric of unusualMetrics(scored)) {\n    out.push(\"- **\" + metric.name + \"** ΓÇö \" + metric.gloss);\n    out.push(\"  \" + metric.honestNote);\n  }\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  /* ---- detail ---- */\n  out.push(\"## Per-case detail\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(...extractionDetail(scored));\n  out.push(...plantDetail(scored));\n  out.push(...questionDetail(scored));\n  out.push(...guardDetail(scored));\n\n  /* ---- ablation ---- */\n  if (study !== null) {\n    out.push(\"## Ablation study\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(describeStudy(study));\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(renderAblationTable(study));\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    out.push(\"### Prediction audit\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(renderPredictionAudit(study));\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n  } else {\n    out.push(\"## Ablation study\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    out.push(\"NOT RUN. Pass `--ablation` or run `npm run eval:ablation` separately.\");\n    out.push(\"A baseline without ablations supports claims about what the system scores, not about which\");\n    out.push(\"component earned the score ΓÇö and only the second kind of claim is an architectural one.\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  /* ---- hardening ---- */\n  out.push(\"## Hardening pass\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  if (hardening === null) {\n    out.push(\"NOT RUN. `npm run eval` computes it; a report without it has checked no demo-killer.\");\n  } else {\n    out.push(\"```\");\n    out.push(renderHardening(hardening));\n    out.push(\"```\");\n  }\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  /* ---- new territory ---- */\n  out.push(\"## Metrics with no published comparator\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  const without = metricsWithoutComparator(scored);\n  out.push(\n    without.length + \" of \" + scored.metrics.length +\n    \" metrics have no published comparator. This is the new-territory claim stated as a count rather than\",\n    \"as an adjective:\",\n  );\n  out.push(\"\");\n  for (const metric of without) out.push(\"- \" + metric.name);\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  return out.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Sections                                                                   */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nfunction describeEngine(scored: ScoredRun): string {\n  const first = scored.metrics[0]?.perEngine[0];\n  return first === undefined ? \"unknown\" : first.engine;\n}\n\nfunction metricsSummary(scored: ScoredRun): string {\n  const lines = [\n    \"Thirteen metrics over \" + scored.run.transcripts.length + \" transcripts. Every one carries its sample\",\n    \"size, its comparator where one is published, and a statement of what it does not establish.\",\n    \"\",\n  ];\n\n  for (const metric of scored.metrics) {\n    const value = metric.id === \"false-positive-rate\"\n      ? metric.value.toFixed(1) + \" per 100 (lower is better)\"\n      : metric.value.toFixed(3);\n    lines.push(\"- **\" + metric.name + \"**: \" + value + \" (n=\" + metric.n + \")\");\n  }\n\n  return lines.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\nfunction metricSection(metric: MetricResult): string[] {\n  const out = [\"#### \" + metric.name, \"\"];\n  const detail = metric.detail;\n\n  out.push(\n    \"**\" + (metric.id === \"false-positive-rate\" ? metric.value.toFixed(1) + \" per 100 decisions\" : metric.value.toFixed(3)) +\n    \"** ┬╖ n=\" + metric.n +\n    (detail === null ? \"\" : \" ┬╖ P \" + detail.precision.toFixed(3) + \" / R \" + detail.recall.toFixed(3) +\n      \" / F1 \" + detail.f1.toFixed(3) + \" (TP \" + detail.truePositives + \", FP \" + detail.falsePositives + \", FN \" + detail.falseNegatives + \")\"),\n  );\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(metric.gloss);\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"Comparator: \" + (metric.comparator === null\n    ? \"**none published.** The absence is the finding ΓÇö see the honest note.\"\n    : metric.comparator.source + \" ΓÇö \" + metric.comparator.value));\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"> \" + metric.honestNote);\n  out.push(\"\");\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction extractionDetail(scored: ScoredRun): string[] {\n  const out = [\"### Extraction\", \"\"];\n\n  out.push(\"| transcript | TP | FP | FN |\");\n  out.push(\"| --- | --- | --- | --- |\");\n  for (const entry of scored.extraction.perTranscript) {\n    out.push(\"| \" + entry.transcript + \" | \" + entry.tp + \" | \" + entry.fp + \" | \" + entry.fn + \" |\");\n  }\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  if (scored.extraction.variableErrors.length > 0) {\n    out.push(\n      \"Found the sentence, understood it as the wrong kind of decision (\" +\n      scored.extraction.variableErrors.length + \"):\",\n    );\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const entry of scored.extraction.variableErrors.slice(0, 12)) {\n      out.push(\"- `\" + entry.label.key + \"` labelled \" + entry.label.variable + \", extracted as \" + entry.node.variable);\n    }\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  if (scored.premise.predicateErrors.length > 0) {\n    out.push(\"Premises matched on span but given the wrong predicate (\" + scored.premise.predicateErrors.length + \"):\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const entry of scored.premise.predicateErrors.slice(0, 12)) {\n      out.push(\"- `\" + entry.label.key + \"` labelled `\" + entry.label.predicate + \"`, extracted as `\" + entry.node.predicate + \"`\");\n    }\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  if (scored.premise.typeErrors.length > 0) {\n    out.push(\n      \"Premise TYPE errors (\" + scored.premise.typeErrors.length + \") ΓÇö spans overlap but the types disagree.\",\n      \"The substrate forbids merging across types, so these are near-misses the alignment refused:\",\n    );\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const entry of scored.premise.typeErrors.slice(0, 12)) {\n      out.push(\"- `\" + entry.label.key + \"` labelled \" + entry.label.type + \", extracted as \" + entry.node.type);\n    }\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  if (scored.modality.wronglyPromoted.length > 0) {\n    out.push(\"**Statements that should not have become nodes, and did** (\" + scored.modality.wronglyPromoted.length + \"):\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const entry of scored.modality.wronglyPromoted) {\n      out.push(\"- `\" + entry.label.key + \"` (\" + entry.label.modality + \") ΓåÆ \" + entry.node.id);\n    }\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  out.push(\"Alternative FP:FN ratio ΓÇö \" + scored.alternative.fpToFnRatio + \" (ARGUS published 13:1).\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction plantDetail(scored: ScoredRun): string[] {\n  const out = [\"### Planted cases\", \"\"];\n\n  out.push(\"| plant | testable | expected flips | flipped | missed | survivors kept | over-retracted | correct |\");\n  out.push(\"| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\");\n  for (const entry of scored.cascade.perPlant) {\n    out.push(\n      \"| \" + entry.plant + \" | \" + (entry.testable ? \"yes\" : \"NO\") + \" | \" + entry.expectedFlips.length + \" | \" +\n      entry.flipped.length + \" | \" + entry.missedFlips.length + \" | \" + entry.survived.length + \" | \" +\n      entry.overRetracted.length + \" | \" + (entry.correct ? \"Γ£ô\" : \"Γ£ù\") + \" |\",\n    );\n  }\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  if (scored.cascade.overRetractions > 0) {\n    out.push(\"**Over-retractions** ΓÇö each one is the demo-killer ┬º7.4 names first:\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const entry of scored.cascade.perPlant) {\n      for (const key of entry.overRetracted) out.push(\"- \" + entry.plant + \" retracted `\" + key + \"`, which shares no falsified premise\");\n    }\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  if (scored.resolver.unresolvable.length > 0) {\n    out.push(\n      \"**Plant keys with no runtime node** (\" + scored.resolver.unresolvable.length + \"). These plants are scored as\",\n      \"FAILED rather than skipped ΓÇö skipping would make cascade correctness improve as extraction worsens:\",\n    );\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const key of scored.resolver.unresolvable) out.push(\"- \" + key);\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  out.push(\"Contradiction records by gate: \" +\n    scored.contradiction.byGate.structural + \" structural, \" +\n    scored.contradiction.byGate.semantic + \" semantic, \" +\n    scored.contradiction.byGate.premise + \" premise. Kantara diagnostics: \" +\n    scored.contradiction.m1Detections + \" M1, \" + scored.contradiction.m2Detections + \" M2.\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"Justifications carrying a non-empty OUT-list: \" + scored.invalidation.outListPopulated +\n    \" of \" + scored.invalidation.decisionsTotal + \" decisions. Zero means the graph is monotonic and L2 cannot score.\");\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  if (scored.falsePositives.examples.length > 0) {\n    out.push(\"Unexpected interruptions (the false-positive numerator), first \" + scored.falsePositives.examples.length + \":\");\n    out.push(\"\");\n    for (const example of scored.falsePositives.examples) out.push(\"- \" + example);\n    out.push(\"\");\n  }\n\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction questionDetail(scored: ScoredRun): string[] {\n  const out = [\"### Questions\", \"\", \"| id | verdict | expected | groundedness | claims | searches | over budget |\", \"| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\"];\n\n  for (const entry of scored.groundedness.perQuestion) {\n    out.push(\n      \"| \" + entry.question + \" | \" + entry.verdict + \" | \" + entry.expectedVerdict + \" | \" +\n      entry.groundedness.toFixed(2) + \" | \" + entry.groundedClaims + \"/\" + entry.claims + \" | \" +\n      entry.searches + \" | \" + (entry.overBudget ? \"YES\" : \"no\") + \" |\",\n    );\n  }\n  out.push(\"\");\n\n  out.push(\"| id | relevant expected | found in top 5 | first relevant rank |\");\n  out.push(\"| --- | --- | --- | --- |\");\n  for (const entry of scored.retrieval.perQuestion) {\n    out.push(\n      \"| \" + entry.question + \" | \" + entry.relevant.length + \" | \" + entry.relevantFound + \" | \" +\n      (entry.firstRelevantRank === null ? \"ΓÇö\" : String(entry.firstRelevantRank)) + \" |\",\n    );\n  }\n  out.push(\"\");\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction guardDetail(scored: ScoredRun): string[] {\n  const out = [\"### Guards\", \"\"];\n  out.push(\n    \"Precision is NOT MEASURED ΓÇö a transcript corpus contains no repository state, so no violation could be\",\n    \"seeded. See `docs/LIMITATIONS.md`. What is measurable:\",\n  );\n  out.push(\"\");\n  out.push(\"- synthesis rate: \" + scored.guards.decisionsWithGuard + \" of \" + scored.guards.decisionsTotal + \" decisions got a proposed guard\");\n  out.push(\"- determinism share: \" + scored.guards.deterministic + \" deterministic, \" + scored.guards.judged + \" `llm_judge`\");\n  out.push(\"- skips counted as passes (must be 0): \" + scored.guards.skippedCountedAsPass);\n  out.push(\"- guards auto-accepted without a human (must be 0, invariant I4): \" + scored.guards.autoAccepted);\n  out.push(\"- stale `disabledByDecisionLabel` flags (must be 0): \" + scored.guards.staleDisabledFlags);\n  out.push(\"\");\n  return out;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* CLI                                                                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n  const args = readArgs(process.argv.slice(2));\n  const { corpus: resolved, load, engine, engineId } = prepare();\n\n  banner(\"Generating the evaluation report\");\n  line(describeCorpus(resolved));\n  blank();\n  line(describeLoad(load));\n\n  heading(\"Baseline run\");\n  const run = await runConfig(resolved, { id: \"full\", engine, engineId, toggles: FULL, params: DEFAULT_PARAMS });\n  line(\"imported in \" + (run.durationMs / 1000).toFixed(1) + \"s ┬╖ \" + run.state.decisions.size + \" decisions\");\n\n  const scored = scoreAll(resolved, run, engineId);\n\n  let study: AblationStudy | null = null;\n  if (args.has(\"ablation\")) {\n    heading(\"Ablation study\");\n    study = await runAblation(resolved, { engine, engineId });\n    line(describeStudy(study));\n  }\n\n  const hardened = harden(resolved, scored);\n  const markdown = renderFullReport(resolved, scored, study, hardened);\n  write(args.get(\"out\") ?? \"docs/generated/EVAL-REPORT.md\", markdown);\n\n  blank();\n  line(\"The report is generated. docs/EVALUATION.md quotes from it and never restates it, because two\");\n  line(\"copies of a number drift and a benchmark that disagrees with its own output is worse than none.\");\n}\n\nmain().catch((error: unknown) => {\n  process.stderr.write(\"\\n\" + (error instanceof Error ? error.stack ?? error.message : String(error)) + \"\\n\");\n  process.exitCode = 1;\n});\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 29 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 29 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 29 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\extraction\\review\\diff.ts"},"span":[3111,3124],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Commit preview ΓÇö 1.9's \"what will this do?\".\n *\n * Phase 1 ┬╖ sub-phase 1.9\n * Plan: plan/phase-1-extraction-pipeline.md ┬º1.9\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY A PREVIEW EXISTS BEFORE A COMMIT\n *\n * \"The system proposes, the human disposes\" (invariant I4) is only real if the\n * human can see the proposal. A commit button that appends nine events to a\n * hash-chained log without saying which nine is a button the user learns not to\n * press ΓÇö and then the review queue becomes a formality.\n *\n * The preview is also where the idempotence claim becomes VISIBLE. Re-import the\n * same transcript and the preview reads \"0 new nodes ┬╖ 9 merges onto existing\n * nodes\". That sentence is the acceptance criterion for sub-phase 1.7, shown to the\n * user rather than asserted in a test they will never read.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHAT THIS IS NOT\n *\n * Not the Phase 2 cascade preview. This shows what the COMMIT writes. The cascade\n * preview shows what a premise invalidation would retract, and it needs the\n * dependency-directed backtracker, which does not exist yet. Confusing the two\n * would mean promising a cascade prediction the system cannot make.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { DiscardedCandidate } from \"@/ai/types\";\nimport { isAccepted } from \"@/extraction/types\";\nimport type { ResolvedCandidate, ReviewAction } from \"@/extraction/types\";\nimport type { AlternativeId, DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\n\nexport interface CommitPreview {\n  readonly newDecisions: number;\n  readonly mergedDecisions: number;\n  readonly uncertainDecisions: number;\n  readonly newPremises: number;\n  readonly mergedPremises: number;\n  readonly derivedPremises: number;\n  readonly newAlternatives: number;\n  readonly alternativesWithReason: number;\n  readonly discarded: number;\n  readonly edited: number;\n  readonly conflicts: readonly DecisionId[];\n  /** Nodes the commit will merge onto, so the preview can name them. */\n  readonly mergeTargets: readonly {\n    readonly id: DecisionId | PremiseId | AlternativeId;\n    readonly label: string;\n  }[];\n  /** Events the commit will append. Shown as a count because the list is long and\n   *  the number is what tells the user how big the act is. */\n  readonly events: number;\n  readonly actions: Readonly<Record<ReviewAction[\"type\"], number>>;\n  /** Items parked in the Contested queue. Not written as nodes, not thrown away. */\n  readonly contested: number;\n}\n\nconst NO_ACTIONS: Readonly<Record<ReviewAction[\"type\"], number>> = {\n  approve: 0,\n  edit: 0,\n  discard: 0,\n  \"merge-into\": 0,\n  contest: 0,\n};\n\n/**\n * What committing this review would write.\n *\n * Items with no action are NOT counted: an unreviewed item is not committed, and a\n * preview that included it would overstate the act. The review is all-or-nothing\n * per item, and the count of unreviewed items is reported separately so the user\n * knows what they are leaving behind.\n */\nexport function previewCommit(\n  candidates: readonly ResolvedCandidate[],\n  discards: readonly DiscardedCandidate[] = [],\n): CommitPreview {\n  const actions: Record<ReviewAction[\"type\"], number> = { ...NO_ACTIONS };\n  const mergeTargets: { id: DecisionId | PremiseId | AlternativeId; label: string }[] = [];\n  const conflicts = new Set<DecisionId>();\n\n  let newDecisions = 0;\n  let mergedDecisions = 0;\n  let uncertainDecisions = 0;\n  let newPremises = 0;\n  let mergedPremises = 0;\n  let derivedPremises = 0;\n  let newAlternatives = 0;\n  let alternativesWithReason = 0;\n  let edited = 0;\n  let contested = 0;\n\n  for (const candidate of candidates) {\n    const action = candidate.action;\n    if (action === null) continue;\n\n    actions[action.type] += 1;\n    if (action.type === \"edit\") edited += 1;\n    if (!isAccepted(action)) {\n      if (action.type === \"contest\") contested += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    for (const id of candidate.resolution.conflictsWith) conflicts.add(id);\n\n    if (candidate.resolution.action === \"merge\" && candidate.resolution.matchedId !== null) {\n      mergeTargets.push({\n        id: candidate.resolution.matchedId,\n        label: labelFor(candidate),\n      });\n    }\n\n    switch (candidate.kind) {\n      case \"decision\": {\n        if (candidate.resolution.action === \"merge\") mergedDecisions += 1;\n        else if (candidate.resolution.action === \"candidate\") uncertainDecisions += 1;\n        else newDecisions += 1;\n        break;\n      }\n      case \"premise\": {\n        if (candidate.resolution.action === \"merge\") mergedPremises += 1;\n        else newPremises += 1;\n        if (candidate.premise?.text.includes(\"ΓÇö excluded by\")) {\n          derivedPremises += 1;\n        }\n        break;\n      }\n      case \"alternative\": {\n        newAlternatives += 1;\n        if (candidate.alternative?.whyRejected !== undefined) alternativesWithReason += 1;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  const accepted = actions.approve + actions.edit + actions[\"merge-into\"];\n\n  return {\n    newDecisions,\n    mergedDecisions,\n    uncertainDecisions,\n    newPremises,\n    mergedPremises,\n    derivedPremises,\n    newAlternatives,\n    alternativesWithReason,\n    discarded: actions.discard + discards.length,\n    contested,\n    edited,\n    conflicts: [...conflicts],\n    mergeTargets,\n    // One event per accepted node, one per discard, plus the single\n    // ExtractionReviewed that records the whole act. The reason field on state-\n    // changing events is written by the commit, not counted here.\n    events: accepted + actions.discard + discards.length + 1,\n    actions,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction labelFor(candidate: ResolvedCandidate): string {\n  if (candidate.kind === \"decision\" && candidate.decision !== null) {\n    return `${candidate.decision.variable} ΓåÆ ${candidate.decision.value}`;\n  }\n  if (candidate.kind === \"premise\" && candidate.premise !== null) {\n    return `${candidate.premise.type}.${candidate.premise.predicate}`;\n  }\n  if (candidate.kind === \"alternative\" && candidate.alternative !== null) {\n    return `rejected ${candidate.alternative.value}`;\n  }\n  return candidate.key;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Wording                                                                    */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * The paragraph the confirm dialog shows.\n *\n * Ordered by consequence: what gets created, what merges, what is thrown away, and\n * last what conflicts ΓÇö because a conflict is the only line that should make the\n * user stop and read.\n */\nexport function describeCommit(preview: CommitPreview, unreviewed: number): string {\n  if (preview.events === 1) {\n    return unreviewed > 0\n      ? \"Nothing reviewed. Committing now would append one event recording that this extraction was reviewed and everything in it left undecided.\"\n      : \"Nothing to commit.\";\n  }\n\n  const lines: string[] = [];\n\n  const created: string[] = [];\n  if (preview.newDecisions > 0)\n    created.push(`${preview.newDecisions} new decision${plural(preview.newDecisions)}`);\n  if (preview.newPremises > 0)\n    created.push(`${preview.newPremises} new premise${plural(preview.newPremises)}`);\n  if (preview.newAlternatives > 0)\n    created.push(\n      `${preview.newAlternatives} rejected alternative${plural(preview.newAlternatives)}`,\n    );\n  if (created.length > 0) lines.push(`Creates ${created.join(\", \")}.`);\n\n  const merged = preview.mergedDecisions + preview.mergedPremises;\n  if (merged > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `Merges ${merged} onto existing node${plural(merged)} ΓÇö corroboration increments, evidence extends, and no duplicate is created.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.derivedPremises > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.derivedPremises} of those premises ${preview.derivedPremises === 1 ? \"is\" : \"are\"} OUT-list entries inferred from another premise rather than quoted from the transcript.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.alternativesWithReason < preview.newAlternatives) {\n    const without = preview.newAlternatives - preview.alternativesWithReason;\n    lines.push(\n      `${without} alternative${plural(without)} ${without === 1 ? \"has\" : \"have\"} no recovered reason: stored, but excluded from pre-emption, so they will never warn you about anything.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.discarded > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `Discards ${preview.discarded}, recorded as ExtractionDiscarded so the precision denominator counts them.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.contested > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.contested} item${plural(preview.contested)} are CONTESTED: parked rather than written, because a newcomer that cannot outrank every contradictor is not silently applied. Phase 2 turns these into revision proposals.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.edited > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.edited} item${plural(preview.edited)} carry your wording at human_override authority (0.9), not the machine's.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.uncertainDecisions > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.uncertainDecisions} decision${plural(preview.uncertainDecisions)} the identity ladder was unsure about ΓÇö committing creates them as new nodes flagged needsReview.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.conflicts.length > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `CONFLICTS with ${preview.conflicts.join(\", \")} on the same variable with a different value. Committing does not resolve it; Phase 2's contradiction detector will, and until then both stay live.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (unreviewed > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${unreviewed} item${plural(unreviewed)} are unreviewed and will NOT be committed. They stay in this run until you decide or clear it.`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  lines.push(`Appends ${preview.events} events to the log.`);\n  return lines.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/** The one-line version, for the button label and the toast. */\nexport function summariseCommit(preview: CommitPreview): string {\n  const parts: string[] = [];\n  if (preview.newDecisions > 0) parts.push(`${preview.newDecisions} new`);\n  if (preview.mergedDecisions + preview.mergedPremises > 0) {\n    parts.push(`${preview.mergedDecisions + preview.mergedPremises} merged`);\n  }\n  if (preview.discarded > 0) parts.push(`${preview.discarded} discarded`);\n  if (parts.length === 0) return \"nothing reviewed\";\n  return `${parts.join(\" ┬╖ \")} ΓåÆ ${preview.events} events`;\n}\n\nfunction plural(count: number): string {\n  return count === 1 ? \"\" : \"s\";\n}\n\n/** Are there merge targets worth naming? The preview lists up to this many so the\n *  user can see WHICH nodes absorb the new evidence. */\nexport const MERGE_TARGETS_SHOWN = 4;\n\nexport function describeMergeTargets(preview: CommitPreview): string {\n  if (preview.mergeTargets.length === 0) return \"\";\n  const shown = preview.mergeTargets.slice(0, MERGE_TARGETS_SHOWN);\n  const rest = preview.mergeTargets.length - shown.length;\n  const list = shown.map((target) => `${target.id} (${target.label})`).join(\", \");\n  return rest > 0 ? `${list} and ${rest} more` : list;\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 20 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 20 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 20 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\extraction\\review\\diff.ts"},"span":[6713,6727],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Commit preview ΓÇö 1.9's \"what will this do?\".\n *\n * Phase 1 ┬╖ sub-phase 1.9\n * Plan: plan/phase-1-extraction-pipeline.md ┬º1.9\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY A PREVIEW EXISTS BEFORE A COMMIT\n *\n * \"The system proposes, the human disposes\" (invariant I4) is only real if the\n * human can see the proposal. A commit button that appends nine events to a\n * hash-chained log without saying which nine is a button the user learns not to\n * press ΓÇö and then the review queue becomes a formality.\n *\n * The preview is also where the idempotence claim becomes VISIBLE. Re-import the\n * same transcript and the preview reads \"0 new nodes ┬╖ 9 merges onto existing\n * nodes\". That sentence is the acceptance criterion for sub-phase 1.7, shown to the\n * user rather than asserted in a test they will never read.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHAT THIS IS NOT\n *\n * Not the Phase 2 cascade preview. This shows what the COMMIT writes. The cascade\n * preview shows what a premise invalidation would retract, and it needs the\n * dependency-directed backtracker, which does not exist yet. Confusing the two\n * would mean promising a cascade prediction the system cannot make.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { DiscardedCandidate } from \"@/ai/types\";\nimport { isAccepted } from \"@/extraction/types\";\nimport type { ResolvedCandidate, ReviewAction } from \"@/extraction/types\";\nimport type { AlternativeId, DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\n\nexport interface CommitPreview {\n  readonly newDecisions: number;\n  readonly mergedDecisions: number;\n  readonly uncertainDecisions: number;\n  readonly newPremises: number;\n  readonly mergedPremises: number;\n  readonly derivedPremises: number;\n  readonly newAlternatives: number;\n  readonly alternativesWithReason: number;\n  readonly discarded: number;\n  readonly edited: number;\n  readonly conflicts: readonly DecisionId[];\n  /** Nodes the commit will merge onto, so the preview can name them. */\n  readonly mergeTargets: readonly {\n    readonly id: DecisionId | PremiseId | AlternativeId;\n    readonly label: string;\n  }[];\n  /** Events the commit will append. Shown as a count because the list is long and\n   *  the number is what tells the user how big the act is. */\n  readonly events: number;\n  readonly actions: Readonly<Record<ReviewAction[\"type\"], number>>;\n  /** Items parked in the Contested queue. Not written as nodes, not thrown away. */\n  readonly contested: number;\n}\n\nconst NO_ACTIONS: Readonly<Record<ReviewAction[\"type\"], number>> = {\n  approve: 0,\n  edit: 0,\n  discard: 0,\n  \"merge-into\": 0,\n  contest: 0,\n};\n\n/**\n * What committing this review would write.\n *\n * Items with no action are NOT counted: an unreviewed item is not committed, and a\n * preview that included it would overstate the act. The review is all-or-nothing\n * per item, and the count of unreviewed items is reported separately so the user\n * knows what they are leaving behind.\n */\nexport function previewCommit(\n  candidates: readonly ResolvedCandidate[],\n  discards: readonly DiscardedCandidate[] = [],\n): CommitPreview {\n  const actions: Record<ReviewAction[\"type\"], number> = { ...NO_ACTIONS };\n  const mergeTargets: { id: DecisionId | PremiseId | AlternativeId; label: string }[] = [];\n  const conflicts = new Set<DecisionId>();\n\n  let newDecisions = 0;\n  let mergedDecisions = 0;\n  let uncertainDecisions = 0;\n  let newPremises = 0;\n  let mergedPremises = 0;\n  let derivedPremises = 0;\n  let newAlternatives = 0;\n  let alternativesWithReason = 0;\n  let edited = 0;\n  let contested = 0;\n\n  for (const candidate of candidates) {\n    const action = candidate.action;\n    if (action === null) continue;\n\n    actions[action.type] += 1;\n    if (action.type === \"edit\") edited += 1;\n    if (!isAccepted(action)) {\n      if (action.type === \"contest\") contested += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    for (const id of candidate.resolution.conflictsWith) conflicts.add(id);\n\n    if (candidate.resolution.action === \"merge\" && candidate.resolution.matchedId !== null) {\n      mergeTargets.push({\n        id: candidate.resolution.matchedId,\n        label: labelFor(candidate),\n      });\n    }\n\n    switch (candidate.kind) {\n      case \"decision\": {\n        if (candidate.resolution.action === \"merge\") mergedDecisions += 1;\n        else if (candidate.resolution.action === \"candidate\") uncertainDecisions += 1;\n        else newDecisions += 1;\n        break;\n      }\n      case \"premise\": {\n        if (candidate.resolution.action === \"merge\") mergedPremises += 1;\n        else newPremises += 1;\n        if (candidate.premise?.text.includes(\"ΓÇö excluded by\")) {\n          derivedPremises += 1;\n        }\n        break;\n      }\n      case \"alternative\": {\n        newAlternatives += 1;\n        if (candidate.alternative?.whyRejected !== undefined) alternativesWithReason += 1;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  const accepted = actions.approve + actions.edit + actions[\"merge-into\"];\n\n  return {\n    newDecisions,\n    mergedDecisions,\n    uncertainDecisions,\n    newPremises,\n    mergedPremises,\n    derivedPremises,\n    newAlternatives,\n    alternativesWithReason,\n    discarded: actions.discard + discards.length,\n    contested,\n    edited,\n    conflicts: [...conflicts],\n    mergeTargets,\n    // One event per accepted node, one per discard, plus the single\n    // ExtractionReviewed that records the whole act. The reason field on state-\n    // changing events is written by the commit, not counted here.\n    events: accepted + actions.discard + discards.length + 1,\n    actions,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction labelFor(candidate: ResolvedCandidate): string {\n  if (candidate.kind === \"decision\" && candidate.decision !== null) {\n    return `${candidate.decision.variable} ΓåÆ ${candidate.decision.value}`;\n  }\n  if (candidate.kind === \"premise\" && candidate.premise !== null) {\n    return `${candidate.premise.type}.${candidate.premise.predicate}`;\n  }\n  if (candidate.kind === \"alternative\" && candidate.alternative !== null) {\n    return `rejected ${candidate.alternative.value}`;\n  }\n  return candidate.key;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Wording                                                                    */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * The paragraph the confirm dialog shows.\n *\n * Ordered by consequence: what gets created, what merges, what is thrown away, and\n * last what conflicts ΓÇö because a conflict is the only line that should make the\n * user stop and read.\n */\nexport function describeCommit(preview: CommitPreview, unreviewed: number): string {\n  if (preview.events === 1) {\n    return unreviewed > 0\n      ? \"Nothing reviewed. Committing now would append one event recording that this extraction was reviewed and everything in it left undecided.\"\n      : \"Nothing to commit.\";\n  }\n\n  const lines: string[] = [];\n\n  const created: string[] = [];\n  if (preview.newDecisions > 0)\n    created.push(`${preview.newDecisions} new decision${plural(preview.newDecisions)}`);\n  if (preview.newPremises > 0)\n    created.push(`${preview.newPremises} new premise${plural(preview.newPremises)}`);\n  if (preview.newAlternatives > 0)\n    created.push(\n      `${preview.newAlternatives} rejected alternative${plural(preview.newAlternatives)}`,\n    );\n  if (created.length > 0) lines.push(`Creates ${created.join(\", \")}.`);\n\n  const merged = preview.mergedDecisions + preview.mergedPremises;\n  if (merged > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `Merges ${merged} onto existing node${plural(merged)} ΓÇö corroboration increments, evidence extends, and no duplicate is created.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.derivedPremises > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.derivedPremises} of those premises ${preview.derivedPremises === 1 ? \"is\" : \"are\"} OUT-list entries inferred from another premise rather than quoted from the transcript.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.alternativesWithReason < preview.newAlternatives) {\n    const without = preview.newAlternatives - preview.alternativesWithReason;\n    lines.push(\n      `${without} alternative${plural(without)} ${without === 1 ? \"has\" : \"have\"} no recovered reason: stored, but excluded from pre-emption, so they will never warn you about anything.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.discarded > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `Discards ${preview.discarded}, recorded as ExtractionDiscarded so the precision denominator counts them.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.contested > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.contested} item${plural(preview.contested)} are CONTESTED: parked rather than written, because a newcomer that cannot outrank every contradictor is not silently applied. Phase 2 turns these into revision proposals.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.edited > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.edited} item${plural(preview.edited)} carry your wording at human_override authority (0.9), not the machine's.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.uncertainDecisions > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${preview.uncertainDecisions} decision${plural(preview.uncertainDecisions)} the identity ladder was unsure about ΓÇö committing creates them as new nodes flagged needsReview.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (preview.conflicts.length > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `CONFLICTS with ${preview.conflicts.join(\", \")} on the same variable with a different value. Committing does not resolve it; Phase 2's contradiction detector will, and until then both stay live.`,\n    );\n  }\n  if (unreviewed > 0) {\n    lines.push(\n      `${unreviewed} item${plural(unreviewed)} are unreviewed and will NOT be committed. They stay in this run until you decide or clear it.`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  lines.push(`Appends ${preview.events} events to the log.`);\n  return lines.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/** The one-line version, for the button label and the toast. */\nexport function summariseCommit(preview: CommitPreview): string {\n  const parts: string[] = [];\n  if (preview.newDecisions > 0) parts.push(`${preview.newDecisions} new`);\n  if (preview.mergedDecisions + preview.mergedPremises > 0) {\n    parts.push(`${preview.mergedDecisions + preview.mergedPremises} merged`);\n  }\n  if (preview.discarded > 0) parts.push(`${preview.discarded} discarded`);\n  if (parts.length === 0) return \"nothing reviewed\";\n  return `${parts.join(\" ┬╖ \")} ΓåÆ ${preview.events} events`;\n}\n\nfunction plural(count: number): string {\n  return count === 1 ? \"\" : \"s\";\n}\n\n/** Are there merge targets worth naming? The preview lists up to this many so the\n *  user can see WHICH nodes absorb the new evidence. */\nexport const MERGE_TARGETS_SHOWN = 4;\n\nexport function describeMergeTargets(preview: CommitPreview): string {\n  if (preview.mergeTargets.length === 0) return \"\";\n  const shown = preview.mergeTargets.slice(0, MERGE_TARGETS_SHOWN);\n  const rest = preview.mergeTargets.length - shown.length;\n  const list = shown.map((target) => `${target.id} (${target.label})`).join(\", \");\n  return rest > 0 ? `${list} and ${rest} more` : list;\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 27 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 27 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 27 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\feedback.ts"},"span":[7831,7847],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Guards as evidence ΓÇö 4.5. Γÿà\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.5\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.5\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º6\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THIS IS WHAT MAKES GUARDS PART OF THE REASONING CORE\n *\n * A linter reports. This feeds back:\n *\n *   PASS  ΓåÆ corroboration +1 for the decision, and `implementation_cost` becomes a\n *           MEASUREMENT rather than a guess, which raises entrenchment E(d).\n *   FAIL  ΓåÆ a `GuardFailed` event, then the SAME `assertNewInformation` entry point a\n *           transcript uses ΓÇö so the reasoning code never learns that a guard arrived.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE ENTRY POINT IS SHARED, AND THAT IS NOT A CONVENIENCE\n *\n * `reasoning/assert.ts` was written in Phase 2 with four assertion kinds and one rule: the\n * `switch` on `source` lives THERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, and everything downstream sees a\n * `GraphState` and nothing else. There is no `if (source === \"guard\")` in the labeller, the\n * gates, backtracking, cascade, entrenchment, adjudication or proposals.\n *\n * That is why a guard failure and a transcript remark are EQUIVALENT in the eyes of the\n * reasoner, and why `implementation_cost` can feed entrenchment without a special case. A\n * system that handled guards separately would have two reasoning paths, and only one of them\n * would be exercised.\n *\n * So this file produces an `Assertion` of kind `guard-result`. It does not call `revise`, does\n * not touch labels, and does not know which gate will fire. It supplies evidence; Phase 2\n * disposes of it.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHICH GATE FIRES IS DECIDED HERE, AND CONSERVATIVELY\n *\n *   does this falsify a premise the decision rests on?\n *     YES ΓåÆ gate (c): premise invalidation ΓåÆ cascade\n *     NO  ΓåÆ gate (a): structural drift ΓÇö the code disagrees with the decision directly\n *\n * The test is vocabulary overlap between what the guard checked and the text of each premise\n * the decision rests on. It is answered only when EXACTLY ONE premise matches. Zero matches\n * means the failure is about the decision, not its assumptions, so gate (a) takes it. Several\n * matches mean the run cannot tell which assumption broke ΓÇö and guessing would produce\n * precisely the \"confident, spectacular, entirely wrong cascade\" ┬º2.5 warns about, so the\n * ambiguity is reported and gate (a) handles it as drift instead.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * DRIFT IS DETECTED WITH NO SEMANTIC MODEL AT ALL\n *\n * Gate (a) plus a failing guard is a fully deterministic contradiction detector: the ontology\n * says these two values are mutually exclusive, and a dependency check says both are in the\n * tree. No embedding, no threshold, no model. That is the strongest available answer to \"is\n * this an LLM wrapper?\" ΓÇö and it is why the six deterministic guard kinds matter more than the\n * two flexible ones.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { Assertion, PremiseAssertion } from \"@/reasoning/assert\";\nimport type { GuardStatus } from \"@/reasoning/entrenchment\";\nimport type { GraphState } from \"@/substrate/graph-state\";\nimport type { Actor } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { DecisionId, GuardId, ISODateString, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport { asConfidence } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { EventType, NewEvent } from \"@/types/events\";\nimport type { Guard, GuardRunResult } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport { isDeterministic } from \"@/types/guards\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* implementation_cost ΓÇö a measurement, not a guess                           */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * The `GuardStatus` entrenchment reads.\n *\n * Returns `undefined` when the decision has no accepted guard, which is what makes\n * `implementation_cost` fall back to 0.5 and be reported as UNKNOWN. Absent is not zero: a\n * decision nobody has checked is not a decision that costs nothing to retract, and scoring it\n * as cheap would make every unverified decision the easiest one to overturn.\n *\n * Only ACCEPTED guards count, and only ones that actually ran. A proposed guard measures\n * nothing, and a skipped one measured nothing either ΓÇö counting a skip as a pass would raise\n * entrenchment on the strength of data nobody pasted.\n */\nexport function guardStatusFor(state: GraphState, decisionId: DecisionId): GuardStatus | undefined {\n  const decision = state.decisions.get(decisionId);\n  if (decision === undefined) return undefined;\n\n  let total = 0;\n  let passing = 0;\n\n  for (const guardId of decision.guardIds) {\n    const guard = state.guards.get(guardId);\n    if (guard === undefined) continue;\n    if (guard.lifecycle !== \"accepted\") continue;\n    if (guard.disabledByDecisionLabel) continue;\n    if (guard.lastRun === undefined) continue;\n    if (guard.lastRun.outcome === \"skip\") continue;\n\n    total += 1;\n    if (guard.lastRun.outcome === \"pass\") passing += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (total === 0) return undefined;\n\n  return { passingFraction: passing / total, total, passing };\n}\n\n/** The map `ReviseInput.guards` expects, for every live decision. */\nexport function guardStatusMap(state: GraphState): ReadonlyMap<DecisionId, GuardStatus> {\n  const out = new Map<DecisionId, GuardStatus>();\n\n  for (const decision of state.decisions.values()) {\n    if (decision.status === \"superseded\" || decision.status === \"archived\") continue;\n\n    const status = guardStatusFor(state, decision.id);\n    if (status === undefined) continue;\n    out.set(decision.id, status);\n  }\n\n  return out;\n}\n\n/**\n * How many decisions in the log now have a MEASURED implementation cost.\n *\n * This is the sentence Phase 4 exists to make possible: before guards, every decision's cost was\n * 0.5 UNKNOWN; after, the ones with passing guards have a number with evidence behind it.\n */\nexport function measuredCostCoverage(state: GraphState): { measured: number; unknown: number } {\n  let measured = 0;\n  let unknown = 0;\n\n  for (const decision of state.decisions.values()) {\n    if (decision.status === \"superseded\" || decision.status === \"archived\") continue;\n    if (guardStatusFor(state, decision.id) === undefined) unknown += 1;\n    else measured += 1;\n  }\n\n  return { measured, unknown };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Which premise, if any, the failure falsifies                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface PremiseAttribution {\n  /** Set only when EXACTLY ONE premise matched. */\n  readonly premiseId: PremiseId | null;\n  readonly candidates: readonly PremiseId[];\n  /** \"single\" ΓåÆ gate (c). \"none\" or \"ambiguous\" ΓåÆ gate (a) handles it as structural drift. */\n  readonly verdict: \"single\" | \"none\" | \"ambiguous\";\n  readonly explanation: string;\n}\n\n/** Minimum shared content tokens between what the guard checked and a premise's text. Two, for\n *  the same reason retrieval uses two: a single shared token (\"app\", \"server\", \"deploy\") would\n *  attribute a failure to half the log's premises. */\nexport const MIN_ATTRIBUTION_TOKENS = 2;\n\n/**\n * Does this failure falsify a premise the decision rests on?\n *\n * Compares the guard's TARGET ΓÇö the package, glob or pattern it checked, plus the evidence it\n * produced ΓÇö against the text of each IN-list premise. Vocabulary overlap is a weak instrument\n * and is treated as one: the answer is only acted on when exactly one premise matches.\n */\nexport function attributeFailure(\n  state: GraphState,\n  guard: Guard,\n  result: GuardRunResult,\n): PremiseAttribution {\n  const decision = state.decisions.get(guard.decisionId);\n  if (decision === undefined) {\n    return {\n      premiseId: null,\n      candidates: [],\n      verdict: \"none\",\n      explanation:\n        \"The decision this guard protects is not in the log, so there are no premises to attribute the failure to.\",\n    };\n  }\n\n  const targetTokens = tokensOf(`${specTarget(guard)} ${result.evidence}`);\n  const candidates: PremiseId[] = [];\n  const scores: { id: PremiseId; shared: number }[] = [];\n\n  for (const justificationId of decision.justificationIds) {\n    const justification = state.justifications.get(justificationId);\n    if (justification === undefined) continue;\n\n    for (const premiseId of justification.inList) {\n      if (candidates.includes(premiseId)) continue;\n      candidates.push(premiseId);\n\n      const premise = state.premises.get(premiseId);\n      if (premise === undefined) continue;\n\n      const premiseTokens = tokensOf(`${premise.text} ${premise.predicate}`);\n      let shared = 0;\n      for (const token of premiseTokens) {\n        if (targetTokens.has(token)) shared += 1;\n      }\n\n      if (shared >= MIN_ATTRIBUTION_TOKENS) scores.push({ id: premiseId, shared });\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (scores.length === 0) {\n    return {\n      premiseId: null,\n      candidates,\n      verdict: \"none\",\n      explanation: `Nothing the guard checked (${specTarget(guard)}) shares vocabulary with any of the ${candidates.length} premise(s) this decision rests on. The failure is about the DECISION, not its assumptions, so gate (a) treats it as structural drift: the code disagrees with what the log says was chosen.`,\n    };\n  }\n\n  scores.sort((a, b) => b.shared - a.shared || (a.id < b.id ? -1 : 1));\n\n  const best = scores[0];\n  const second = scores[1];\n\n  if (second !== undefined && best !== undefined && second.shared === best.shared) {\n    return {\n      premiseId: null,\n      candidates,\n      verdict: \"ambiguous\",\n      explanation: `The failure matches ${scores.length} premises equally well (${scores.map((entry) => entry.id).join(\", \")}), so the run cannot say which assumption broke. Guessing would produce a confident, spectacular and entirely wrong cascade, so the ambiguity is reported and gate (a) handles it as drift instead. Resolve it by asserting the premise directly.`,\n    };\n  }\n\n  if (best === undefined) {\n    return { premiseId: null, candidates, verdict: \"none\", explanation: \"No premise matched.\" };\n  }\n\n  const premise = state.premises.get(best.id);\n\n  return {\n    premiseId: best.id,\n    candidates,\n    verdict: \"single\",\n    explanation: `The failure falsifies ${best.id} \"${premise?.text ?? \"\"}\" ΓÇö ${best.shared} shared token(s) with what the guard checked, and no other premise matched as well. Gate (c) takes it: the premise goes OUT and every decision resting on it is re-examined.`,\n  };\n}\n\n/** What the guard actually looked at, as text. */\nexport function specTarget(guard: Guard): string {\n  const spec = guard.spec;\n\n  switch (spec.kind) {\n    case \"dependency_present\":\n    case \"dependency_absent\":\n      return spec.package;\n    case \"path_glob_exists\":\n    case \"path_glob_absent\":\n      return spec.glob;\n    case \"string_in_files\":\n    case \"string_absent_in_files\":\n      return `${spec.pattern} ${spec.glob}`;\n    case \"command_exit_zero\":\n      return spec.command;\n    case \"llm_judge\":\n      return `${spec.rubric} ${spec.scopeGlobs.join(\" \")}`;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction tokensOf(text: string): ReadonlySet<string> {\n  return new Set(\n    text\n      .toLowerCase()\n      .split(/[^a-z0-9]+/)\n      .filter((token) => token.length > 2),\n  );\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Turning a run into evidence                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface FeedbackInput {\n  readonly state: GraphState;\n  readonly guard: Guard;\n  readonly result: GuardRunResult;\n  readonly actor: Actor;\n  readonly at: ISODateString;\n}\n\nexport interface GuardFeedback {\n  /** Verification events: `GuardRan` always, `GuardFailed` instead when it failed. */\n  readonly drafts: readonly NewEvent<EventType>[];\n  /** The reasoning assertion. Null when there is nothing to assert ΓÇö a skip, or a pass, which\n   *  corroborates through the guard record rather than through a premise. */\n  readonly assertion: Assertion | null;\n  readonly attribution: PremiseAttribution | null;\n  readonly summary: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * One guard result ΓåÆ verification events + a reasoning assertion.\n *\n * Two kinds of output, deliberately separate:\n *\n *   `drafts`     what the LOG needs to remember: that the guard ran, what it found, and when it\n *                first started failing. These are verification facts.\n *   `assertion`  what the REASONER needs: an observation about the world, in the same shape a\n *                transcript produces. The store feeds this to `assertNewInformation`.\n *\n * Keeping them apart is what lets the guard record be complete even when the reasoning produces\n * nothing ΓÇö a passing guard is worth recording and asserts no premise.\n */\nexport function feedbackFor(input: FeedbackInput): GuardFeedback {\n  const { guard, result } = input;\n\n  /* ---- a skip is not evidence ---- */\n  if (result.outcome === \"skip\") {\n    return {\n      drafts: [ranEvent(input)],\n      assertion: null,\n      attribution: null,\n      summary: `${guard.id} skipped: ${result.skipReason ?? \"no reason given\"} Nothing is asserted, because a guard that could not run says nothing about the world.`,\n    };\n  }\n\n  /* ---- a pass corroborates, through the guard record ---- */\n  if (result.outcome === \"pass\") {\n    return {\n      drafts: [ranEvent(input)],\n      assertion: null,\n      attribution: null,\n      summary: `${guard.id} passed for ${guard.decisionId}. Corroboration and \\`implementation_cost\\` are recomputed from the guard record ΓÇö no premise is asserted, because a check confirming what the log already says is not new information.`,\n    };\n  }\n\n  /* ---- a failure is evidence, and it enters the same door as a transcript ---- */\n  const attribution = attributeFailure(input.state, guard, result);\n  const premise =\n    attribution.premiseId === null ? null : input.state.premises.get(attribution.premiseId);\n\n  const observation: PremiseAssertion = {\n    /* A failure attributed to a premise refutes THAT premise, using its own type and predicate\n     * so the resolver finds the existing node rather than minting a duplicate ΓÇö the Phase 2\n     * audit's first and worst gap. An unattributed failure asserts a premise about the decision\n     * itself, which gate (a) then reads as drift. */\n    type: premise?.type ?? \"requirement\",\n    predicate: premise?.predicate ?? driftPredicate(guard),\n    text: observationText(guard, result, attribution.verdict),\n    /* False: the guard's finding REFUTES what the log believed. */\n    supports: false,\n    /* A deterministic guard is direct observation of the project, so it is the most confident\n     * evidence source in the system ΓÇö more confident than a human remark, which can be\n     * misremembered, and incomparably more than a model's reading. A judged guard is not: it is\n     * a model's opinion, and it gets the confidence of one. */\n    confidence: asConfidence(\n      isDeterministic(guard.spec.kind) ? GUARD_CONFIDENCE : JUDGED_CONFIDENCE,\n    ),\n  };\n\n  const assertion: Assertion = {\n    source: \"guard-result\",\n    decisionId: guard.decisionId,\n    passed: false,\n    observation,\n  };\n\n  /* `actor` and `reason` are mandatory on every `NewEvent`, and `GuardFailed` is one of the 19\n   * state-changing types whose reason the event log rejects when empty. The reason names the\n   * guard, the decision and the finding, because this is the field whose whole job is to say\n   * where the information came from ΓÇö and a guard failure arriving with a generic reason is\n   * indistinguishable, in the audit trail, from one that was asserted by hand. */\n  const drafts: NewEvent<EventType>[] = [\n    {\n      type: \"GuardFailed\",\n      payload: {\n        guardId: guard.id,\n        result: \"fail\",\n        evidence: result.evidence,\n        ranAt: result.ranAt,\n        firstFailedAt: guard.firstFailedAt ?? result.ranAt,\n        ...(attribution.premiseId === null ? {} : { falsifiesPremiseId: attribution.premiseId }),\n      },\n      actor: input.actor,\n      reason: `Guard ${guard.id} (${guard.spec.kind}) failed while protecting ${guard.decisionId}: ${result.evidence}${\n        attribution.premiseId === null\n          ? \" No premise matched, so this is recorded as drift against the decision itself.\"\n          : ` Attributed to premise ${attribution.premiseId}.`\n      }`,\n      at: input.at,\n      occurredAt: result.ranAt,\n    },\n  ];\n\n  return {\n    drafts,\n    assertion,\n    attribution,\n    summary: `${guard.id} FAILED for ${guard.decisionId}. Evidence: ${result.evidence} ${attribution.explanation}`,\n  };\n}\n\n/** The predicate for an unattributed failure. Namespaced under `drift` so the resolver cannot\n *  confuse it with an extracted premise, and so a drift observation is recognisable in the log\n *  as something a check produced rather than something a person said. */\nfunction driftPredicate(guard: Guard): string {\n  return `drift.${guard.spec.kind}.${slug(specTarget(guard))}`;\n}\n\nfunction slug(text: string): string {\n  return text\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"_\")\n    .replace(/^_+|_+$/g, \"\")\n    .slice(0, 40);\n}\n\n/** What the guard observed, as a sentence. This becomes premise text, so it has to read as an\n *  observation about the world and not as a log message. */\nfunction observationText(\n  guard: Guard,\n  result: GuardRunResult,\n  verdict: PremiseAttribution[\"verdict\"],\n): string {\n  const target = specTarget(guard);\n\n  const observed =\n    guard.spec.kind === \"dependency_absent\" ||\n    guard.spec.kind === \"path_glob_absent\" ||\n    guard.spec.kind === \"string_absent_in_files\"\n      ? `the project now contains ${target}`\n      : `the project no longer contains ${target}`;\n\n  return `${observed} ΓÇö observed by guard ${guard.id} (${guard.spec.kind}), which ${guard.decisionId} is protected by. Evidence: ${result.evidence}${\n    verdict === \"none\"\n      ? \" This is drift against the decision itself, not against any premise it rests on.\"\n      : verdict === \"ambiguous\"\n        ? \" Which premise this falsifies could not be determined, so it is reported as drift.\"\n        : \"\"\n  }`;\n}\n\nfunction ranEvent(input: FeedbackInput): NewEvent<EventType> {\n  return {\n    type: \"GuardRan\",\n    payload: {\n      guardId: input.guard.id,\n      result: input.result.outcome,\n      evidence: input.result.evidence,\n      ranAt: input.result.ranAt,\n    },\n    actor: input.actor,\n    /* `GuardRan` is not state-changing, so the log would accept an empty reason ΓÇö but a run\n     * recorded without saying what it found is a run nobody can audit later, and the field\n     * costs nothing to fill. */\n    reason: `Guard ${input.guard.id} ran against the pasted project and ${\n      input.result.outcome === \"pass\"\n        ? `passed: ${input.result.evidence}`\n        : input.result.outcome === \"skip\"\n          ? `SKIPPED: ${input.result.skipReason ?? \"no reason given\"}`\n          : `failed: ${input.result.evidence}`\n    }`,\n    at: input.at,\n    occurredAt: input.result.ranAt,\n  };\n}\n\n/** A deterministic guard is direct observation; a judged one is an opinion. */\nexport const GUARD_CONFIDENCE = 0.9;\nexport const JUDGED_CONFIDENCE = 0.4;\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Reporting                                                                  */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport function describeFeedbackLoop(): string {\n  return [\n    \"Guards are evidence, not lint. A run feeds back two ways:\",\n    \"\",\n    \"  PASS   corroboration +1, and `implementation_cost` becomes a MEASUREMENT ΓÇö the fraction\",\n    \"         of the decision's accepted guards that ran and passed. That raises entrenchment\",\n    \"         E(d), so a decision with six passing guards is correctly expensive to retract.\",\n    \"         A decision with NO accepted guard stays at 0.5 and is reported as UNKNOWN, never\",\n    \"         as 'medium': a missing measurement must not masquerade as a measurement.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"  FAIL   a GuardFailed event, then the SAME assertNewInformation entry point a transcript\",\n    \"         uses. The reasoning code never learns a guard arrived ΓÇö there is no\",\n    '         `if (source === \"guard\")` downstream, and there cannot be, because the switch on',\n    \"         source lives in reasoning/assert.ts and nowhere else.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"           does it falsify a premise the decision rests on?\",\n    \"             YES (exactly one match) ΓåÆ gate (c) ΓåÆ premise OUT ΓåÆ cascade\",\n    \"             NO or AMBIGUOUS         ΓåÆ gate (a) ΓåÆ structural drift ΓåÆ reversal proposal\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Ambiguity is reported, never guessed: a wrong premise attribution produces a confident,\",\n    \"spectacular and entirely wrong cascade.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Consequence worth stating plainly: gate (a) plus a failing guard is a fully deterministic\",\n    `contradiction detector. No embedding, no threshold, no model. Confidence ${GUARD_CONFIDENCE} for a deterministic guard (direct observation) against ${JUDGED_CONFIDENCE} for a judged one (an opinion).`,\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\nexport type { GuardId, NewEvent };\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 21 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 21 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 21 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\synth\\llm.ts"},"span":[5620,5628],"sourceCode":"/**\n * LLM guard synthesis ΓÇö 4.2. For what the templates miss.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.2\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.2\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * EVERY GUARD FROM HERE IS FLAGGED `requiresReview`\n *\n * Not as a courtesy ΓÇö as the mechanism that keeps ┬º4.2's first rule true. A model proposing a\n * check is a guess about what the decision means in code, and a guess that is installed\n * unreviewed is a guard that fails on day one, which is the failure that gets the whole panel\n * ignored.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE MODEL MAY ONLY PROPOSE KINDS THE INTERPRETER CAN RUN\n *\n * A proposed spec is validated against the eight-kind union and rejected if it does not fit:\n * unknown kind, empty package, empty glob, empty pattern. This is not distrust of the model so\n * much as distrust of JSON ΓÇö a hallucinated field produces a guard that evaluates to something\n * nobody intended, and \"pass\" is the most dangerous thing it could produce.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * NO KEY, NO GUESS\n *\n * When the engine cannot synthesise, this returns an empty set and a REASON. The reason is\n * carried into the `unverifiable` explanation, so a decision that could have been checked with\n * a key says so, rather than being marked unverifiable as though nothing could ever check it.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { PremiseSnapshot, WhyLogEngine } from \"@/ai/types\";\nimport { asConfidence } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { Decision } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport type {\n  CommandSpec,\n  DependencySpec,\n  GuardSeverity,\n  GuardSpec,\n  LlmJudgeSpec,\n  PathGlobSpec,\n  ProposedGuard,\n  StringSpec,\n} from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport { DEFAULT_SEVERITY } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { TemplateInput } from \"../types\";\n\nexport interface LlmSynthInput {\n  readonly engine: WhyLogEngine;\n  readonly decision: Decision;\n  readonly templateInput: TemplateInput;\n  /** What the templates already proposed, so the model is asked for something DIFFERENT rather\n   *  than for a second opinion on a check that already exists. */\n  readonly alreadyProposed: readonly ProposedGuard[];\n  /** The decision's REAL premises, with their real ids. Passed in rather than reconstructed\n   *  from `templateInput.premises`, whose texts have no ids attached ΓÇö inventing ids here would\n   *  put fabricated `P-000n` strings into a model prompt, and a model that quotes one back\n   *  would be citing a premise that does not exist. */\n  readonly premises: readonly PremiseSnapshot[];\n  readonly slotsRemaining: number;\n  readonly signal?: AbortSignal;\n}\n\nexport interface LlmSynthResult {\n  readonly guards: readonly ProposedGuard[];\n  /** Why nothing came back, when nothing did. Carried into the unverifiable explanation. */\n  readonly reason: string | null;\n}\n\nexport async function llmGuards(input: LlmSynthInput): Promise<LlmSynthResult> {\n  if (input.slotsRemaining <= 0) {\n    return { guards: [], reason: \"not consulted ΓÇö the templates already filled the cap.\" };\n  }\n\n  if (\n    !input.engine.capabilities().guardSynthesis ||\n    input.engine.capabilities().guardSynthesis === \"none\"\n  ) {\n    return {\n      guards: [],\n      reason:\n        \"the active engine cannot synthesise guards. This decision may be checkable with an LLM engine configured in Settings, but nothing was proposed without one.\",\n    };\n  }\n\n  const result = await input.engine.synthesizeGuards({\n    decisionId: input.decision.id,\n    variable: input.decision.variable,\n    value: input.decision.value,\n    statement: input.decision.statement,\n    appliesTo: input.decision.appliesTo,\n    premises: input.premises,\n    rejected: input.templateInput.rejected,\n    ...(input.signal === undefined ? {} : { signal: input.signal }),\n  });\n\n  if (result.guards.length === 0) {\n    return {\n      guards: [],\n      reason: result.unverifiableReason ?? \"the engine proposed nothing and gave no reason.\",\n    };\n  }\n\n  const existing = new Set(input.alreadyProposed.map((guard) => describeSpec(guard.spec)));\n  const out: ProposedGuard[] = [];\n\n  for (const proposed of result.guards) {\n    if (out.length >= input.slotsRemaining) break;\n\n    const validated = validate(proposed);\n    if (validated === null) continue;\n\n    /* Skip a proposal that duplicates something the templates already produced. Two guards for\n     * the same check crowd out a different check under the cap. */\n    const key = describeSpec(validated.spec);\n    if (existing.has(key)) continue;\n    existing.add(key);\n\n    out.push({\n      ...validated,\n      /* Forced, whatever the engine claimed. See the header: this flag is the mechanism that\n       * keeps guards proposed rather than installed, and it is not the model's to remove. */\n      requiresReview: true,\n      generatedBy: \"llm\",\n    });\n  }\n\n  return { guards: out, reason: null };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Validation                                                                 */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * Accept a proposed guard only if its spec is one the interpreter can actually run.\n *\n * Returns null ΓÇö dropping the proposal ΓÇö rather than repairing it. A repaired spec is a check\n * nobody proposed, and a guard that silently evaluates something other than what was asked for\n * is worse than no guard.\n */\nexport function validate(proposed: ProposedGuard): ProposedGuard | null {\n  const spec = proposed.spec;\n\n  switch (spec.kind) {\n    case \"dependency_present\":\n    case \"dependency_absent\": {\n      if (spec.package.trim().length === 0) return null;\n      const fixed: DependencySpec = {\n        kind: spec.kind,\n        package: spec.package.trim(),\n        ...(spec.includeDev === undefined ? {} : { includeDev: spec.includeDev }),\n      };\n      return withSpec(proposed, fixed);\n    }\n\n    case \"path_glob_exists\":\n    case \"path_glob_absent\": {\n      if (spec.glob.trim().length === 0) return null;\n      const fixed: PathGlobSpec = { kind: spec.kind, glob: spec.glob.trim() };\n      return withSpec(proposed, fixed);\n    }\n\n    case \"string_in_files\":\n    case \"string_absent_in_files\": {\n      if (spec.pattern.trim().length === 0 || spec.glob.trim().length === 0) return null;\n      const fixed: StringSpec = {\n        kind: spec.kind,\n        pattern: spec.pattern,\n        glob: spec.glob.trim(),\n        /* Defaults forced to the safe side: word boundaries ON, because a bare substring match\n         * on \"pg\" hits \"pgp\" and \"pgsql-tools\", which is the documented false-positive mode. */\n        wordBoundary: spec.wordBoundary ?? true,\n        caseSensitive: spec.caseSensitive ?? false,\n      };\n      return withSpec(proposed, fixed);\n    }\n\n    case \"command_exit_zero\": {\n      if (spec.command.trim().length === 0) return null;\n      const fixed: CommandSpec = {\n        kind: spec.kind,\n        command: spec.command.trim(),\n        ...(spec.cwd === undefined ? {} : { cwd: spec.cwd }),\n      };\n      return withSpec(proposed, fixed);\n    }\n\n    case \"llm_judge\": {\n      if (spec.rubric.trim().length === 0 || spec.scopeGlobs.length === 0) return null;\n      const fixed: LlmJudgeSpec = {\n        kind: spec.kind,\n        rubric: spec.rubric.trim(),\n        scopeGlobs: spec.scopeGlobs.filter((glob) => glob.trim().length > 0),\n      };\n      if (fixed.scopeGlobs.length === 0) return null;\n      return withSpec(proposed, fixed);\n    }\n\n    default:\n      /* An unknown kind. TypeScript says this is unreachable; JSON from a model says otherwise,\n       * and this is the branch that catches it. */\n      return null;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction withSpec(proposed: ProposedGuard, spec: GuardSpec): ProposedGuard {\n  const severity: GuardSeverity = proposed.severity === \"blocking\" ? \"blocking\" : \"warning\";\n\n  return {\n    spec,\n    /* A rationale is mandatory (┬º4.1): a guard without a stated reason is indistinguishable\n     * from a lint rule. A model that proposed none gets a placeholder that says so, rather than\n     * the guard being dropped ΓÇö the reviewer can then write the reason, which is a better\n     * outcome than losing a check that was probably right. */\n    rationale:\n      proposed.rationale.trim().length > 0\n        ? proposed.rationale.trim()\n        : \"Proposed by the model with no stated rationale. Write one before accepting: a guard without a reason is indistinguishable from a lint rule.\",\n    /* A model-proposed guard never gets `blocking` by default, whatever it asked for. Blocking\n     * fails a build, and a guess should not be able to do that until a human has read it. */\n    severity: severity === \"blocking\" ? DEFAULT_SEVERITY[spec.kind] : severity,\n    generatedBy: \"llm\",\n    confidence: asConfidence(Math.min(proposed.confidence, LLM_CONFIDENCE_CAP)),\n    requiresReview: true,\n  };\n}\n\n/** No model-proposed guard may claim more certainty than a template rule written by a person\n *  who knew what the variable meant. */\nexport const LLM_CONFIDENCE_CAP = 0.6;\n\n/** A readable one-line description of a spec, for de-duplication against the template output. */\nexport function describeSpec(spec: GuardSpec): string {\n  switch (spec.kind) {\n    case \"dependency_present\":\n    case \"dependency_absent\":\n      return `${spec.kind} ${spec.package}`;\n    case \"path_glob_exists\":\n    case \"path_glob_absent\":\n      return `${spec.kind} ${spec.glob}`;\n    case \"string_in_files\":\n    case \"string_absent_in_files\":\n      return `${spec.kind} ${JSON.stringify(spec.pattern)} in ${spec.glob}`;\n    case \"command_exit_zero\":\n      return `${spec.kind} ${spec.command}`;\n    case \"llm_judge\":\n      return `${spec.kind} over ${spec.scopeGlobs.join(\", \")}`;\n  }\n}\n\nexport type { Decision };\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 19 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 19 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 19 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\templates\\hosting.ts"},"span":[2268,2281],"sourceCode":"/**\n * HOSTING templates ΓÇö 4.2.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.2\n *\n * \"HOSTING ΓåÆ config file + deploy manifest\". A hosting decision is the one whose drift is most\n * expensive and least visible: the code keeps working locally while the deploy target quietly\n * changes, and the premises the decision rested on (no deploy infra, single region, free tier)\n * stop being true without anything in the source tree saying so.\n */\n\nimport { asConfidence } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { ProposedGuard } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport { DEFAULT_SEVERITY } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { TemplateInput, TemplateOutput } from \"../types\";\n\ninterface HostingRule {\n  readonly value: string;\n  readonly configFile: string;\n  readonly packages: readonly string[];\n  readonly excludes: readonly string[];\n}\n\nconst RULES: readonly HostingRule[] = [\n  {\n    value: \"vercel\",\n    configFile: \"vercel.json\",\n    packages: [\"vercel\"],\n    excludes: [\"netlify-cli\", \"wrangler\", \"@railway/cli\"],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"netlify\",\n    configFile: \"netlify.toml\",\n    packages: [\"netlify-cli\"],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"wrangler\"],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"cloudflare\",\n    configFile: \"wrangler.toml\",\n    packages: [\"wrangler\"],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"netlify-cli\"],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"railway\",\n    configFile: \"railway.json\",\n    packages: [\"@railway/cli\"],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"netlify-cli\"],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"fly\",\n    configFile: \"fly.toml\",\n    packages: [\"@flydotio/dockerfile\"],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"netlify-cli\"],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"aws\",\n    configFile: \"**/*serverless.yml\",\n    packages: [\"aws-sdk\", \"@aws-sdk/client-s3\"],\n    excludes: [],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"self-hosted\",\n    configFile: \"docker-compose.yml\",\n    packages: [],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"netlify-cli\", \"wrangler\"],\n  },\n  { value: \"docker\", configFile: \"Dockerfile\", packages: [], excludes: [] },\n  { value: \"neon\", configFile: \"\", packages: [\"@neondatabase/serverless\"], excludes: [] },\n  {\n    value: \"local\",\n    configFile: \"\",\n    packages: [],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"netlify-cli\", \"wrangler\", \"@railway/cli\"],\n  },\n  {\n    value: \"none\",\n    configFile: \"\",\n    packages: [],\n    excludes: [\"vercel\", \"netlify-cli\", \"wrangler\", \"@railway/cli\"],\n  },\n];\n\nexport function hostingGuards(input: TemplateInput): TemplateOutput {\n  const rule = RULES.find((candidate) => candidate.value === input.value.toLowerCase());\n  if (rule === undefined) return [];\n\n  const out: ProposedGuard[] = [];\n\n  if (rule.configFile.length > 0) {\n    out.push({\n      spec: { kind: \"path_glob_exists\", glob: rule.configFile },\n      rationale: `${input.decisionId} chose ${input.value} for hosting, so a \\`${rule.configFile}\\` deploy manifest should exist. Hosting is the decision whose drift is most expensive and least visible: the code keeps working locally while the deploy target changes underneath it.`,\n      severity: DEFAULT_SEVERITY.path_glob_exists,\n      generatedBy: \"template\",\n      confidence: asConfidence(0.6),\n      requiresReview: false,\n    });\n  }\n\n  for (const pkg of rule.packages) {\n    out.push({\n      spec: { kind: \"dependency_present\", package: pkg, includeDev: true },\n      rationale: `\\`${pkg}\\` is how ${input.value} hosting shows up in a dependency tree, and ${input.decisionId} settled on it.`,\n      severity: DEFAULT_SEVERITY.dependency_present,\n      generatedBy: \"template\",\n      confidence: asConfidence(0.6),\n      requiresReview: false,\n    });\n  }\n\n  /* A LOCAL / no-hosting decision is the interesting case: every deploy CLI is evidence\n   * against it. That is the guard which catches \"we said this was a desktop app\" drifting into\n   * \"someone added a Vercel deploy on Friday\". */\n  for (const excluded of rule.excludes) {\n    out.push({\n      spec: { kind: \"dependency_absent\", package: excluded, includeDev: true },\n      rationale: `${input.decisionId} settled hosting on ${input.value}${\n        isUnhosted(input.value)\n          ? `, which means no deploy tooling at all. \\`${excluded}\\` appearing would mean the project has acquired a deploy target the log does not know about ΓÇö and every premise about scale, cost or single-user access that the decision rested on would need re-examining.`\n          : `, so \\`${excluded}\\` should not be present.`\n      }`,\n      severity: DEFAULT_SEVERITY.dependency_absent,\n      generatedBy: \"template\",\n      confidence: asConfidence(isUnhosted(input.value) ? 0.8 : 0.6),\n      requiresReview: false,\n    });\n  }\n\n  for (const rejected of input.rejected) {\n    const rejectedRule = RULES.find(\n      (candidate) => candidate.value === rejected.value.toLowerCase(),\n    );\n    if (rejectedRule === undefined) continue;\n\n    const pkg = rejectedRule.packages[0];\n    if (\n      pkg !== undefined &&\n      !out.some((g) => g.spec.kind === \"dependency_absent\" && g.spec.package === pkg)\n    ) {\n      out.push({\n        spec: { kind: \"dependency_absent\", package: pkg, includeDev: true },\n        rationale: `${input.decisionId} rejected ${rejected.value} hosting${rejected.whyRejected === undefined ? \".\" : ` ΓÇö \"${rejected.whyRejected}\".`}`,\n        severity: DEFAULT_SEVERITY.dependency_absent,\n        generatedBy: \"template\",\n        confidence: asConfidence(0.75),\n        requiresReview: false,\n      });\n    }\n\n    if (rejectedRule.configFile.length > 0) {\n      out.push({\n        spec: { kind: \"path_glob_absent\", glob: rejectedRule.configFile },\n        rationale: `A \\`${rejectedRule.configFile}\\` would mean ${rejected.value} hosting was configured despite ${input.decisionId} rejecting it.`,\n        severity: DEFAULT_SEVERITY.path_glob_absent,\n        generatedBy: \"template\",\n        confidence: asConfidence(0.6),\n        requiresReview: false,\n      });\n    }\n  }\n\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction isUnhosted(value: string): boolean {\n  const folded = value.toLowerCase();\n  return (\n    folded === \"local\" || folded === \"none\" || folded === \"self-hosted\" || folded === \"desktop\"\n  );\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 16 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 16 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 16 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\synth\\index.ts"},"span":[7428,7444],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The synthesis pipeline ΓÇö 4.2.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.2\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.2\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º4\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * COMPILE A DECISION INTO A GUARD SET\n *\n * Phase 1 produced a decision in a structured enough form to generate from ΓÇö variable, value,\n * rejected alternatives, `applies_to` globs, premises. In every workflow the fitness-function\n * literature surveys, a human writes the check by hand, because nobody has the decision in that\n * form. This is the part that is nearly free once you do.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * TWO RULES THAT KEEP IT HONEST\n *\n * **1. Guards are PROPOSED, never auto-installed.** Every guard leaves this pipeline with\n * `lifecycle: \"proposed\"`, and `eval/index.ts` refuses to run a proposed guard. A synthesised\n * guard that fails on day one is worse than no guard, because it teaches the user to ignore the\n * panel ΓÇö and a panel that gets ignored stops being verification.\n *\n * **2. A decision with no synthesizable guard is marked `unverifiable`, never silently\n * skipped.** The literature's own test ΓÇö \"can I describe what a violation looks like?\" ΓÇö becomes\n * a visible property of the log. Unverifiable decisions are exactly the ones most likely to\n * drift, so hiding them is the worst available option, and a compliance score computed only over\n * the decisions that happened to be checkable is a score that flatters itself.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * TEMPLATES FIRST, MODEL SECOND, AND THE RATIO IS MEASURED\n *\n * Templates cover the decision deterministically and are tried first. The model is consulted\n * only for what they miss, and everything it produces is flagged `requiresReview`. The\n * acceptance criterion is that at least 80% of proposed guards come from templates ΓÇö so\n * `SynthesisReport` counts both, and the health panel prints the ratio rather than asserting it.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE CAP IS APPLIED HERE, RANKED BY DETERMINISM\n *\n * Three guards per decision (┬º4.7). What gets dropped is always the least trustworthy kind,\n * never whichever happened to be generated last ΓÇö `llm_judge` loses to `dependency_absent`\n * every time, which is the correct order for a system whose headline metric is deterministic.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { PremiseSnapshot, WhyLogEngine } from \"@/ai/types\";\nimport type { GraphState } from \"@/substrate/graph-state\";\nimport type { DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { Decision } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport type { ProposedGuard, UnverifiableDecision } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport { type AttributedGuard, templateGuards } from \"../templates\";\nimport {\n  GUARDS_PER_DECISION_CAP,\n  type SynthesisReport,\n  type TemplateInput,\n  kindRank,\n} from \"../types\";\nimport { llmGuards } from \"./llm\";\n\nexport interface SynthesizeInput {\n  readonly state: GraphState;\n  readonly decisionId: DecisionId;\n  readonly engine?: WhyLogEngine | null;\n  readonly cap?: number;\n  /** Force templates only, even when an engine is available. Used to measure the template\n   *  coverage claim on its own, and by the keyless demo path. */\n  readonly templatesOnly?: boolean;\n  readonly signal?: AbortSignal;\n}\n\n/**\n * Compile one decision into a proposed guard set.\n *\n * Never throws. A decision with nothing checkable returns an empty set plus an\n * `UnverifiableDecision` saying why ΓÇö which is a result, not a failure.\n */\nexport async function synthesize(input: SynthesizeInput): Promise<SynthesisReport> {\n  const started = Date.now();\n  const cap = input.cap ?? GUARDS_PER_DECISION_CAP;\n\n  const decision = input.state.decisions.get(input.decisionId);\n  if (decision === undefined) {\n    return emptyReport(input.decisionId, \"That decision is not in the log.\", started);\n  }\n\n  const templateInput = templateInputFor(input.state, decision);\n  const attributed = templateGuards(templateInput);\n  const fromTemplates = attributed.length;\n\n  let proposals: readonly ProposedGuard[] = attributed.map((entry) => entry.guard);\n  let fromLlm = 0;\n  let llmReason: string | null = null;\n\n  /* ---- the model, only for what the templates missed ---- */\n  const shouldAskModel =\n    input.templatesOnly !== true &&\n    input.engine !== undefined &&\n    input.engine !== null &&\n    attributed.length < cap;\n\n  if (shouldAskModel && input.engine !== undefined && input.engine !== null) {\n    const result = await llmGuards({\n      engine: input.engine,\n      decision,\n      templateInput,\n      premises: premiseSnapshotsFor(input.state, decision),\n      alreadyProposed: proposals,\n      slotsRemaining: Math.max(0, cap - proposals.length),\n      ...(input.signal === undefined ? {} : { signal: input.signal }),\n    });\n\n    fromLlm = result.guards.length;\n    llmReason = result.reason;\n    proposals = [...proposals, ...result.guards];\n  }\n\n  /* ---- cap, ranked by determinism ---- */\n  const ranked = rankForCap(proposals);\n  const capped = ranked.slice(0, cap);\n  const dropped = ranked.length - capped.length;\n\n  const unverifiable: UnverifiableDecision | null =\n    capped.length === 0\n      ? {\n          decisionId: decision.id,\n          reason: unverifiableReason(decision, templateInput, llmReason),\n        }\n      : null;\n\n  return {\n    decisionId: decision.id,\n    proposed: capped,\n    unverifiable,\n    fromTemplates: Math.min(fromTemplates, capped.length),\n    fromLlm: Math.min(fromLlm, Math.max(0, capped.length - fromTemplates)),\n    cappedAt: dropped > 0 ? cap : null,\n    durationMs: Date.now() - started,\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * Synthesise for every live decision in the log.\n *\n * Sequential rather than parallel: on the LLM path each call is a model request, and firing one\n * per decision at once is a rate limit and a bill. Templates are synchronous, so the keyless\n * path costs nothing here.\n */\nexport async function synthesizeAll(\n  input: Omit<SynthesizeInput, \"decisionId\">,\n): Promise<{ reports: readonly SynthesisReport[]; unverifiable: readonly UnverifiableDecision[] }> {\n  const reports: SynthesisReport[] = [];\n  const unverifiable: UnverifiableDecision[] = [];\n\n  for (const decision of input.state.decisions.values()) {\n    if (decision.status === \"superseded\" || decision.status === \"archived\") continue;\n\n    const report = await synthesize({ ...input, decisionId: decision.id });\n    reports.push(report);\n    if (report.unverifiable !== null) unverifiable.push(report.unverifiable);\n  }\n\n  return { reports, unverifiable };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Building the template input                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * The decision, reduced to what a template needs.\n *\n * Built from the graph rather than from the decision record alone: rejected alternatives live\n * in `state.alternatives` and premises live behind the justification set, so a template that\n * only saw the `Decision` would never learn what was turned down ΓÇö and \"we chose X, not Y\" is\n * the single most verifiable shape a decision has.\n */\nexport function templateInputFor(state: GraphState, decision: Decision): TemplateInput {\n  const rejected: { value: string; whyRejected?: string }[] = [];\n\n  for (const alternativeId of decision.alternativeIds) {\n    const alternative = state.alternatives.get(alternativeId);\n    if (alternative === undefined) continue;\n    rejected.push(\n      alternative.whyRejected === undefined\n        ? { value: alternative.value }\n        : { value: alternative.value, whyRejected: alternative.whyRejected },\n    );\n  }\n\n  const premises: string[] = [];\n  const seen = new Set<string>();\n\n  for (const justificationId of decision.justificationIds) {\n    const justification = state.justifications.get(justificationId);\n    if (justification === undefined) continue;\n\n    for (const premiseId of justification.inList) {\n      if (seen.has(premiseId)) continue;\n      seen.add(premiseId);\n      const premise = state.premises.get(premiseId);\n      if (premise === undefined) continue;\n      premises.push(premise.text);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return {\n    decisionId: decision.id,\n    variable: decision.variable,\n    value: decision.value,\n    statement: decision.statement,\n    rejected,\n    appliesTo: decision.appliesTo,\n    premises,\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * The decision's premises as the AI layer sees them.\n *\n * Separate from `templateInputFor` because templates want TEXT (for rationale phrasing) and the\n * engine wants ids, types and predicates. Building both from the same justification walk keeps\n * them consistent without either carrying fields the other does not use.\n */\nexport function premiseSnapshotsFor(\n  state: GraphState,\n  decision: Decision,\n): readonly PremiseSnapshot[] {\n  const out: PremiseSnapshot[] = [];\n  const seen = new Set<PremiseId>();\n\n  for (const justificationId of decision.justificationIds) {\n    const justification = state.justifications.get(justificationId);\n    if (justification === undefined) continue;\n\n    for (const premiseId of justification.inList) {\n      if (seen.has(premiseId)) continue;\n      seen.add(premiseId);\n\n      const premise = state.premises.get(premiseId);\n      if (premise === undefined) continue;\n\n      out.push({\n        id: premiseId,\n        type: premise.type,\n        predicate: premise.predicate,\n        text: premise.text,\n      });\n    }\n  }\n\n  return out;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Ranking and reporting                                                      */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Most deterministic first, then blocking before warning, then higher confidence. This is the\n *  order the cap cuts in, so the guards that survive are the ones whose results can be\n *  trusted without a model. */\nexport function rankForCap(guards: readonly ProposedGuard[]): readonly ProposedGuard[] {\n  return [...guards].sort((a, b) => {\n    const byKind = kindRank(a.spec.kind) - kindRank(b.spec.kind);\n    if (byKind !== 0) return byKind;\n\n    const bySeverity = (a.severity === \"blocking\" ? 0 : 1) - (b.severity === \"blocking\" ? 0 : 1);\n    if (bySeverity !== 0) return bySeverity;\n\n    return b.confidence - a.confidence;\n  });\n}\n\nfunction unverifiableReason(\n  decision: Decision,\n  templateInput: TemplateInput,\n  llmReason: string | null,\n): string {\n  const parts: string[] = [];\n\n  parts.push(\n    `No guard could be synthesised for ${decision.id} (${decision.variable} = ${decision.value}).`,\n  );\n\n  if (templateInput.rejected.length === 0) {\n    parts.push(\n      \"It records no rejected alternative, and a rejection is the most verifiable thing a decision carries ΓÇö whatever the variable, the thing you turned down should not have appeared in the tree since.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (templateInput.appliesTo.length === 0) {\n    parts.push(\"It declares no `applies_to` globs, so there is no scope to check within.\");\n  }\n\n  if (decision.value.trim().length === 0) {\n    parts.push(\"Its value is empty, so there is nothing to look for.\");\n  }\n\n  parts.push(\n    \"The literature's test is whether you can describe what a VIOLATION looks like. For this decision you cannot yet ΓÇö which makes it unverifiable, not fine. Unverifiable decisions are the ones most likely to drift, because nothing would tell you if they did.\",\n  );\n\n  if (llmReason !== null) parts.push(`Model synthesis: ${llmReason}`);\n\n  return parts.join(\" \");\n}\n\nfunction emptyReport(decisionId: DecisionId, reason: string, started: number): SynthesisReport {\n  return {\n    decisionId,\n    proposed: [],\n    unverifiable: { decisionId, reason },\n    fromTemplates: 0,\n    fromLlm: 0,\n    cappedAt: null,\n    durationMs: Date.now() - started,\n  };\n}\n\n/** The ΓëÑ80% claim, measured rather than asserted. */\nexport function templateShare(reports: readonly SynthesisReport[]): {\n  templates: number;\n  llm: number;\n  share: number;\n  meetsTarget: boolean;\n} {\n  let templates = 0;\n  let llm = 0;\n\n  for (const report of reports) {\n    templates += report.fromTemplates;\n    llm += report.fromLlm;\n  }\n\n  const total = templates + llm;\n  const share = total === 0 ? 0 : templates / total;\n\n  return { templates, llm, share, meetsTarget: total > 0 && share >= TEMPLATE_SHARE_TARGET };\n}\n\nexport const TEMPLATE_SHARE_TARGET = 0.8;\n\nexport function describeSynthesis(reports: readonly SynthesisReport[]): string {\n  const share = templateShare(reports);\n  const unverifiable = reports.filter((report) => report.unverifiable !== null);\n  const capped = reports.filter((report) => report.cappedAt !== null);\n\n  return [\n    `${reports.length} decision(s) put through synthesis.`,\n    `  proposed guards   ${reports.reduce((total, report) => total + report.proposed.length, 0)}`,\n    `  from templates    ${share.templates}`,\n    `  from the model    ${share.llm}${share.llm > 0 ? \"  (every one flagged requiresReview)\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  template share    ${(share.share * 100).toFixed(0)}%${share.meetsTarget ? \"  ΓÇö meets the ΓëÑ80% target\" : \"  ΓÇö BELOW the ΓëÑ80% target\"}`,\n    `  unverifiable      ${unverifiable.length}${unverifiable.length > 0 ? `  ΓÇö ${unverifiable.map((report) => report.decisionId).join(\", \")}` : \"\"}`,\n    `  capped at ${GUARDS_PER_DECISION_CAP}        ${capped.length} decision(s) had more proposed than the cap allows; the least deterministic were dropped`,\n    \"\",\n    \"Nothing here is installed. Every guard is `proposed` until accepted on the review list, and\",\n    \"a proposed guard does not run ΓÇö a synthesised guard that fails on day one teaches you to\",\n    \"ignore the panel, which is worse than having no guard at all.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\nexport type { AttributedGuard };\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/useLiteralKeys","severity":"error","description":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Unsafe fix: Use a literal key instead."}]]},{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"]record.dependencies === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,38]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":181}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[38,143]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[143,165]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[165,184]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[184,185]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[185,318]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":455}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[318,336]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[7929,7943],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":["fixable"],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/useLiteralKeys","severity":"error","description":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Unsafe fix: Use a literal key instead."}]]},{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"]record.devDependencies === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,38]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":181}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[38,183]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[183,208]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[208,230]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[230,231]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[231,321]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":455}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[321,339]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[7969,7986],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":["fixable"],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/useLiteralKeys","severity":"error","description":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Unsafe fix: Use a literal key instead."}]]},{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]record.dependencies) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,38]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":196}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[38,89]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[89,111]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[111,130]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[130,227]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":440}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[227,245]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[8440,8454],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":["fixable"],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/useLiteralKeys","severity":"error","description":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"The computed expression can be simplified without the use of a string literal."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Unsafe fix: Use a literal key instead."}]]},{"diff":{"dictionary":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]record.devDependencies) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n","ops":[{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[0,38]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":197}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[38,157]}}},{"diffOp":{"delete":{"range":[157,182]}}},{"diffOp":{"insert":{"range":[182,204]}}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[204,247]}}},{"equalLines":{"line_count":439}},{"diffOp":{"equal":{"range":[247,265]}}}]}}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[8509,8526],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":["fixable"],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 31 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 31 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 31 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[7467,7480],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 17 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 17 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 17 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[12869,12878],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 16 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 16 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 16 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\guards\\project.ts"},"span":[14906,14924],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The virtual project ΓÇö 4.3.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n * Docs: docs/04-executable-decision-guards.md ┬º5.1\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * GUARDS HAVE TO RUN SOMEWHERE, AND HERE THERE IS NO SHELL\n *\n * The app runs in a browser, inside a sandboxed preview, with no filesystem access and no\n * network. A guard system that needed those would not be demonstrable at all.\n *\n * So the user pastes what their project looks like ΓÇö a `package.json`, a file listing from\n * `tree` / `git ls-files` / `find . -type f`, and optionally file contents ΓÇö and the\n * interpreter evaluates real logic against that real data in memory.\n *\n * **This is the designed default and not a compromise.** It is real evaluation: the same\n * predicate, the same evidence string, the same pass/fail the exported CI runner produces. The\n * only thing missing is automatic file discovery, which is a repo-connection concern (v2), not\n * a correctness concern.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE\n *\n * The rule that makes this honest. Paste a `package.json` and nothing else, and:\n *\n *   dependency guards   EVALUATE ΓÇö the manifest is exactly the data they need\n *   path guards         SKIP    ΓÇö there is no listing, so \"no files match\" is unknowable\n *   string guards       SKIP    ΓÇö there are no contents\n *\n * A skip is never a pass. If a `path_glob_absent` guard PASSED against an empty listing it\n * would report success for a codebase it never saw, and the compliance score would be a\n * measurement of how little the user pasted. `GuardRunReport.nothingCheckable` exists so a run\n * that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * UNPARSEABLE LINES ARE KEPT, NOT DROPPED\n *\n * A parse that silently ignores a line produces a project missing a file, and a missing file\n * makes `path_glob_exists` fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the codebase. So every\n * line that could not be interpreted is reported back. A truncated listing is a real hazard for\n * this whole approach and the user is entitled to see its extent.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport { nowISO } from \"@/lib/format\";\nimport { isGlob } from \"@/lib/glob\";\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { VirtualProject, VirtualProjectSource } from \"@/types/guards\";\nimport type { ProjectParseReport } from \"./types\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface ProjectInput {\n  /** A whole `package.json`, or just its dependencies object. */\n  readonly manifest?: string;\n  /** `tree`, `git ls-files`, or `find . -type f` output. */\n  readonly listing?: string;\n  /** File contents, in any of the shapes `parseFileContents` accepts. */\n  readonly contents?: string;\n  readonly source?: VirtualProjectSource;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build the virtual project.\n *\n * Never throws. Malformed JSON, a listing in an unrecognised shape and empty input are all\n * ordinary outcomes that get reported rather than raised: a guard panel that crashes on a bad\n * paste is a panel nobody pastes into twice.\n */\nexport function buildProject(input: ProjectInput): ProjectParseReport {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  let dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let devDependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseManifest(input.manifest);\n    dependencies = parsed.dependencies;\n    devDependencies = parsed.devDependencies;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No package.json was pasted, so dependency guards cannot run.\");\n  }\n\n  let filePaths: readonly string[] = [];\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileListing(input.listing);\n    filePaths = parsed.paths;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      \"No file listing was pasted, so path guards will SKIP rather than pass. Paste the output of `git ls-files`, `find . -type f` or `tree`.\",\n    );\n  }\n\n  let fileContents: Record<string, string> = {};\n  if (input.contents !== undefined && input.contents.trim().length > 0) {\n    const parsed = parseFileContents(input.contents);\n    fileContents = parsed.contents;\n    notices.push(...parsed.notices);\n    unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\"No file contents were pasted, so string guards will SKIP rather than pass.\");\n  }\n\n  /* Paths named in the contents are also paths in the project. A user who pastes contents but\n   * no listing still has a listing, implicitly, and ignoring it would skip the path guards for\n   * no reason. */\n  const implied = Object.keys(fileContents).filter((path) => !filePaths.includes(path));\n  if (implied.length > 0 && filePaths.length === 0) {\n    filePaths = implied;\n    notices.push(\n      `No listing was pasted, so the ${implied.length} file(s) named in the contents are used as the listing. Path guards can run; a file you did not paste is still absent from them.`,\n    );\n  } else if (implied.length > 0) {\n    filePaths = [...filePaths, ...implied];\n  }\n\n  const project: VirtualProject = {\n    dependencies,\n    devDependencies,\n    filePaths,\n    fileContents,\n    sourcedFrom: input.source ?? defaultSource(input),\n    loadedAt: nowISO() as ISODateString,\n  };\n\n  return {\n    project,\n    dependenciesParsed: Object.keys(dependencies).length,\n    devDependenciesParsed: Object.keys(devDependencies).length,\n    filesParsed: filePaths.length,\n    contentsParsed: Object.keys(fileContents).length,\n    unparsed,\n    notices,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction defaultSource(input: ProjectInput): VirtualProjectSource {\n  if (input.manifest !== undefined && input.manifest.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n  if (input.listing !== undefined && input.listing.trim().length > 0)\n    return { kind: \"pasted-file-listing\" };\n  return { kind: \"pasted-manifest\" };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* package.json                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ManifestParse {\n  readonly dependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly devDependencies: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a manifest.\n *\n * Accepts a whole `package.json` OR just the fragment a user is likely to paste ΓÇö a\n * dependencies object on its own, or loose `\"name\": \"version\"` lines. The loose form is\n * supported because someone answering \"does my project have pg in it?\" pastes the bit they\n * were looking at, not the whole file, and rejecting that paste would make the dependency\n * guards ΓÇö the two most reliable kinds in the taxonomy ΓÇö unusable in the demo.\n */\nexport function parseManifest(raw: string): ManifestParse {\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const trimmed = raw.trim();\n\n  /* ---- try it as JSON first ---- */\n  try {\n    const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(trimmed);\n    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\") {\n      const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n\n      /* A bare dependencies object rather than a whole manifest: every value is a string. */\n      if (record[\"dependencies\"] === undefined && record[\"devDependencies\"] === undefined) {\n        const loose = asVersionMap(record);\n        if (loose !== null) {\n          return {\n            dependencies: loose,\n            devDependencies: {},\n            notices: [\n              \"Parsed as a bare dependency map (no `dependencies` wrapper), so all of it is treated as runtime dependencies.\",\n            ],\n            unparsed,\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      return {\n        dependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"dependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        devDependencies: asVersionMap(record[\"devDependencies\"]) ?? {},\n        notices,\n        unparsed,\n      };\n    }\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted manifest parsed as JSON but not as an object, so no dependencies were read.\",\n    );\n  } catch {\n    /* Not JSON ΓÇö fall through to the line-based parse. This is the common case and not an\n     * error: the notices below say what was recovered. */\n  }\n\n  /* ---- fall back to `\"name\": \"version\"` lines ---- */\n  const dependencies: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let section: \"dependencies\" | \"devDependencies\" | null = null;\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const line of trimmed.split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const text = line.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    if (/^\"?devDependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"devDependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (/^\"?dependencies\"?\\s*:/.test(text)) {\n      section = \"dependencies\";\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const match = /^\"?(@?[^\"\\s:]+)\"?\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]*)\"/.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) {\n      /* Only report lines that look like they were MEANT to be dependencies. Braces, commas\n       * and unrelated prose are not worth flagging, and flagging them would bury the one line\n       * that actually matters. */\n      if (text.includes(\":\") && !/^[{}[\\],]+$/.test(text)) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = match[1];\n    const version = match[2];\n    if (name === undefined || version === undefined) continue;\n\n    dependencies[name] = version;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  if (count === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      'The pasted manifest was not JSON and contained no recognisable `\"name\": \"version\"` lines, so no dependencies were read.',\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `The pasted manifest was not valid JSON. Recovered ${count} dependency line(s) by pattern${section === \"devDependencies\" ? \"; the last section seen was devDependencies, but without valid JSON the split cannot be trusted, so all of them are treated as runtime dependencies.\" : \".\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return { dependencies, devDependencies: {}, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\n/** A record whose values are all strings, i.e. a dependency map. */\nfunction asVersionMap(value: unknown): Record<string, string> | null {\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(value)) return null;\n\n  const out: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let count = 0;\n\n  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(value as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n    if (typeof entry !== \"string\") continue;\n    out[key] = entry;\n    count += 1;\n  }\n\n  return count > 0 ? out : null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File listings                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ListingParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a file listing.\n *\n * Three shapes, auto-detected per line:\n *\n *   `tree`          box-drawing prefixes and indentation, directories to reconstruct\n *   `git ls-files`  one clean relative path per line\n *   `find -type f`  one path per line, usually with a `./` prefix\n */\nexport function parseFileListing(raw: string): ListingParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  const looksLikeTree = lines.some((line) => /[Γö£ΓööΓöéΓöÇ]/.test(line));\n  const parsed = looksLikeTree ? parseTree(lines) : parsePlainLines(lines);\n\n  if (parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      looksLikeTree\n        ? \"The listing looked like `tree` output but no file paths could be reconstructed from it.\"\n        : \"No file paths were recognised in the listing.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(\n      `Read ${parsed.paths.length} path(s) from ${looksLikeTree ? \"`tree`-style\" : \"plain\"} listing.${parsed.directoryCount > 0 ? ` ${parsed.directoryCount} directory node(s) were reconstructed and are not listed as files.` : \"\"}`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  unparsed.push(...parsed.unparsed);\n\n  return { paths: parsed.paths, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\ninterface TreeParse {\n  readonly paths: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n  readonly directoryCount: number;\n}\n\n/** Reconstruct full paths from `tree` output by tracking indentation depth. */\nfunction parseTree(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  /* Stack of directory names by depth. `dirs[d]` is the directory name at depth d. */\n  const dirs: string[] = [];\n  let directoryCount = 0;\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    if (raw.trim().length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* The summary line tree prints at the end. */\n    if (/^\\s*\\d+\\s+director/.test(raw)) continue;\n    /* The root line, usually \".\" or a directory name with no prefix. */\n    const prefixMatch = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(raw);\n    const prefix = prefixMatch?.[1] ?? \"\";\n\n    if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(raw) && prefix.trim().length === 0) {\n      /* Root of the tree. Depth 0 with no connector ΓÇö record as the base directory unless it\n       * is \".\" which means \"the project root\" and contributes no path segment. */\n      const root = raw.trim();\n      if (root !== \".\" && root !== \"./\" && root.length > 0) dirs[0] = root;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const name = raw\n      .replace(/[ΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]/g, \" \")\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")\n      .trim();\n    if (name.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* Depth = how many 4-character indent units precede the connector. */\n    const depth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(prefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n\n    const isDir = looksLikeDirectory(name, depth, lines, raw);\n    if (isDir) {\n      dirs[depth] = name;\n      dirs.length = depth + 1;\n      directoryCount += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const parents = dirs\n      .slice(0, depth)\n      .filter((segment) => segment !== undefined && segment !== \".\");\n    out.push([...parents, name].join(\"/\"));\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount };\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this tree node a directory?\n *\n * A node is a directory if some later line is indented deeper than it ΓÇö that is the only\n * reliable signal, because `tree` marks directories by colour, not by punctuation. The\n * extension heuristic is a fallback for the last node in a subtree, which has no deeper line\n * after it.\n */\nfunction looksLikeDirectory(\n  name: string,\n  depth: number,\n  lines: readonly string[],\n  current: string,\n): boolean {\n  const index = lines.indexOf(current);\n  if (index >= 0) {\n    for (let i = index + 1; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n      const next = lines[i];\n      if (next === undefined || next.trim().length === 0) continue;\n      if (!/[Γö£ΓööΓöé]/.test(next)) break;\n\n      const nextPrefix = /^([\\sΓöéΓö£ΓööΓöÇ]*)/.exec(next)?.[1] ?? \"\";\n      const nextDepth = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nextPrefix.replace(/[^Γöé ]/g, \"\").length / 4));\n      if (nextDepth > depth) return true;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /* Fallback: no extension and no dot in the last segment. Wrong for extensionless files like\n   * `Makefile` and `Dockerfile`, so those are special-cased ΓÇö a guard that reports a Makefile\n   * as a directory silently removes it from the listing. */\n  if (EXTENSIONLESS_FILES.has(name.toLowerCase())) return false;\n  return !name.includes(\".\") && !isGlob(name);\n}\n\nconst EXTENSIONLESS_FILES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  \"makefile\",\n  \"dockerfile\",\n  \"license\",\n  \"readme\",\n  \"procfile\",\n  \"gemfile\",\n  \"rakefile\",\n  \"justfile\",\n  \"brewfile\",\n  \"cargo.lock\",\n  \"yarn.lock\",\n  \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n  \".gitignore\",\n  \".editorconfig\",\n  \".nvmrc\",\n  \".env\",\n]);\n\nfunction parsePlainLines(lines: readonly string[]): TreeParse {\n  const out: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const raw of lines) {\n    const text = raw.trim();\n    if (text.length === 0) continue;\n\n    /* `find` prefixes with ./ ; `ls -R` prints \"dir:\" headers; `tree` summaries are prose. */\n    if (/^\\d+\\s+director/.test(text)) continue;\n    if (text.endsWith(\":\") && !text.includes(\"/\")) continue;\n\n    const path = text.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\").replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n\n    if (!looksLikePath(path)) {\n      /* Only flag lines that could plausibly have been paths. Prose in a paste is common ΓÇö\n       * people paste a whole terminal scrollback ΓÇö and flagging every word of it would bury\n       * the one malformed path that matters. */\n      if (path.includes(\"/\") || path.includes(\".\")) unparsed.push(text);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    out.push(path);\n  }\n\n  return { paths: dedupe(out), unparsed, directoryCount: 0 };\n}\n\n/** A path has a separator or an extension, and no whitespace or shell metacharacters. */\nfunction looksLikePath(candidate: string): boolean {\n  if (candidate.length === 0 || candidate.length > 400) return false;\n  if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (/[*?\"<>|]/.test(candidate)) return false;\n  if (candidate.includes(\"/\")) return true;\n  return candidate.includes(\".\") && !candidate.startsWith(\".\");\n}\n\nfunction dedupe(paths: readonly string[]): readonly string[] {\n  return [...new Set(paths)];\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* File contents                                                              */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\ninterface ContentsParse {\n  readonly contents: Record<string, string>;\n  readonly notices: readonly string[];\n  readonly unparsed: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse pasted file contents.\n *\n * Accepts the shapes people actually paste:\n *\n *   `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`      fenced with dashes\n *   `=== src/db/schema.ts ===`      fenced with equals\n *   `## src/db/schema.ts`           markdown heading\n *   `// src/db/schema.ts`           a comment header, when the path looks like one\n *   `src/db/schema.ts:`             a trailing-colon header, as `grep -n` or `head` prints\n *\n * Anything before the first header is treated as a single unnamed blob and reported, not\n * silently discarded.\n */\nexport function parseFileContents(raw: string): ContentsParse {\n  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n  const notices: string[] = [];\n  const unparsed: string[] = [];\n  const contents: Record<string, string> = {};\n\n  let current: string | null = null;\n  let buffer: string[] = [];\n  let headers = 0;\n\n  const flush = (): void => {\n    if (current === null) return;\n    contents[current] = buffer.join(\"\\n\");\n    buffer = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const line of lines) {\n    const header = headerOf(line);\n\n    if (header !== null) {\n      flush();\n      current = header;\n      headers += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (current === null) {\n      /* Content before any header. Kept so it can be reported; assigning it to a guessed path\n       * would invent a file that does not exist. */\n      if (line.trim().length > 0) unparsed.push(line);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    buffer.push(line);\n  }\n\n  flush();\n\n  if (headers === 0) {\n    notices.push(\n      \"The pasted contents had no recognisable file headers, so nothing was attributed to a path. Separate files with a line like `--- src/db/schema.ts ---`.\",\n    );\n  } else {\n    notices.push(`Read contents for ${headers} file(s). String guards can now run against them.`);\n  }\n\n  return { contents, notices, unparsed };\n}\n\nconst HEADER_PATTERNS: readonly RegExp[] = [\n  /^[-=]{2,}\\s*(.+?)\\s*[-=]{2,}$/,\n  /^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*#*$/,\n  /^\\/\\/\\/?\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*$/,\n  /^\\/\\*\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*\\*\\/$/,\n  /^<!--\\s*(\\S+\\.\\S+)\\s*-->/,\n  /^(\\S+\\.\\S+):\\s*$/,\n];\n\n/** Is this line a file header? Returns the path, or null. */\nfunction headerOf(line: string): string | null {\n  const text = line.trim();\n  if (text.length === 0 || text.length > 300) return null;\n\n  for (const pattern of HEADER_PATTERNS) {\n    const match = pattern.exec(text);\n    if (match === null) continue;\n\n    const candidate = match[1]?.trim();\n    if (candidate === undefined) continue;\n    if (!looksLikePath(candidate)) continue;\n\n    /* A header must look like a path AND not like a sentence. `--- this is a note ---` would\n     * otherwise become a file called \"this is a note\", and a phantom path in the listing makes\n     * path guards fail against nothing. */\n    if (/\\s/.test(candidate)) continue;\n\n    return candidate.replace(/^\\.\\//, \"\");\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Project queries ΓÇö what the evaluators actually call                        */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Is a package present? `includeDev` decides whether devDependencies count. */\nexport function findDependency(\n  project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  includeDev: boolean,\n): { version: string; dev: boolean } | null {\n  const runtime = project.dependencies[name];\n  if (runtime !== undefined) return { version: runtime, dev: false };\n\n  if (includeDev) {\n    const dev = project.devDependencies[name];\n    if (dev !== undefined) return { version: dev, dev: true };\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** The exact manifest line for a dependency, quoted verbatim. The evidence string in a\n *  failure comes from here, because evidence without a quote is not evidence. */\nexport function dependencyEvidenceLine(\n  _project: VirtualProject,\n  name: string,\n  found: { version: string; dev: boolean },\n): string {\n  const section = found.dev ? \"devDependencies\" : \"dependencies\";\n  return `\"${name}\": \"${found.version}\" found in package.json ${section}`;\n}\n\n/** Can path guards run at all? False when no listing was pasted. */\nexport function hasFileListing(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return project.filePaths.length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can string guards run at all? False when no contents were pasted. */\nexport function hasFileContents(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return Object.keys(project.fileContents).length > 0;\n}\n\n/** Can dependency guards run at all? */\nexport function hasManifest(project: VirtualProject): boolean {\n  return (\n    Object.keys(project.dependencies).length > 0 || Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length > 0\n  );\n}\n\nexport function describeProject(project: VirtualProject): string {\n  const deps = Object.keys(project.dependencies).length;\n  const dev = Object.keys(project.devDependencies).length;\n  const files = project.filePaths.length;\n  const contents = Object.keys(project.fileContents).length;\n\n  return [\n    `Virtual project ┬╖ loaded ${project.loadedAt} ┬╖ source ${project.sourcedFrom.kind}`,\n    `  dependencies     ${deps} runtime, ${dev} dev${deps + dev === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö dependency guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file listing     ${files} path(s)${files === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö path guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    `  file contents    ${contents} file(s)${contents === 0 ? \"  ΓÇö string guards will SKIP\" : \"\"}`,\n    \"\",\n    \"A skip is never a pass. Guards whose data was not pasted report SKIP with a reason, so a\",\n    \"run that could check nothing cannot be mistaken for a clean run.\",\n  ].join(\"\\n\");\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 19 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 19 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 19 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\lib\\format.ts"},"span":[1555,1569],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Formatting helpers for dates, numbers, and Markdown emission.\n *\n * Phase 5 (export) and Phase 6 (presentation).\n *\n * All date formatting is explicit-locale (\"en-GB\" style ordering is avoided in\n * favour of ISO and long-form) so output is stable across machines. The export\n * compiler's output must be byte-identical for the same graph, or the integrity\n * stamp is meaningless.\n */\n\nimport type { ISODateString } from \"@/types/common\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Dates                                                                      */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport function toISO(date: Date): ISODateString {\n  return date.toISOString() as ISODateString;\n}\n\nexport function nowISO(): ISODateString {\n  return toISO(new Date());\n}\n\nexport function parseISO(value: ISODateString | string): Date | null {\n  const d = new Date(value);\n  return Number.isNaN(d.getTime()) ? null : d;\n}\n\n/** 2026-10-03 */\nexport function formatDate(value: ISODateString | string): string {\n  const d = parseISO(value);\n  if (!d) return String(value);\n  return d.toISOString().slice(0, 10);\n}\n\n/** 3 Oct 2026 */\nexport function formatLongDate(value: ISODateString | string): string {\n  const d = parseISO(value);\n  if (!d) return String(value);\n  return d.toLocaleDateString(\"en-GB\", {\n    day: \"numeric\",\n    month: \"short\",\n    year: \"numeric\",\n    timeZone: \"UTC\",\n  });\n}\n\n/** \"18 days ago\" / \"4 minutes ago\" / \"just now\" */\nexport function formatRelative(value: ISODateString | string, from: Date = new Date()): string {\n  const d = parseISO(value);\n  if (!d) return String(value);\n\n  const seconds = Math.round((from.getTime() - d.getTime()) / 1000);\n  if (seconds < 45) return \"just now\";\n\n  const abs = Math.abs(seconds);\n  const units: [number, string, string][] = [\n    [60, \"second\", \"seconds\"],\n    [3600, \"minute\", \"minutes\"],\n    [86400, \"hour\", \"hours\"],\n    [2592000, \"day\", \"days\"],\n    [31536000, \"month\", \"months\"],\n  ];\n\n  let divisor = 1;\n  let singular = \"second\";\n  let plural = \"seconds\";\n  for (const [limit, s, p] of units) {\n    if (abs < limit * (limit === 60 ? 60 : limit === 3600 ? 60 : limit === 86400 ? 30 : 12)) {\n      divisor = limit;\n      singular = s;\n      plural = p;\n      break;\n    }\n    divisor = limit;\n    singular = s;\n    plural = p;\n  }\n\n  const n = Math.round(abs / divisor);\n  const suffix = seconds < 0 ? \"from now\" : \"ago\";\n  return `${n} ${n === 1 ? singular : plural} ${suffix}`;\n}\n\n/** Whole days between two timestamps. Used by the recency term of entrenchment\n *  and by Drift Age. */\nexport function daysBetween(a: ISODateString | string, b: ISODateString | string): number {\n  const da = parseISO(a);\n  const db = parseISO(b);\n  if (!da || !db) return 0;\n  return Math.abs(db.getTime() - da.getTime()) / 86_400_000;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Numbers                                                                    */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport function formatPercent(value: number, decimals = 0): string {\n  if (!Number.isFinite(value)) return \"ΓÇö\";\n  return `${(value * 100).toFixed(decimals)}%`;\n}\n\nexport function formatScore(value: number, decimals = 2): string {\n  if (!Number.isFinite(value)) return \"ΓÇö\";\n  return value.toFixed(decimals);\n}\n\n/** Rough token estimate for budget indicators: ~4 chars per token. Coarse, but\n *  the budget indicator only needs to be directionally honest. */\nexport function estimateTokens(text: string): number {\n  return Math.ceil(text.length / 4);\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Markdown emission                                                          */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** Escape Markdown control characters in user-authored text. Decision\n *  statements and rationale come from transcripts and routinely contain\n *  backticks, asterisks and angle brackets. */\nexport function escapeMarkdown(input: string): string {\n  return input.replace(/([\\\\`*_{}[\\]<>|])/g, \"\\\\$1\");\n}\n\n/** Inline code, safe for values that may contain backticks. */\nexport function inlineCode(input: string): string {\n  const fence = input.includes(\"`\") ? \"``\" : \"`\";\n  return `${fence}${input}${fence}`;\n}\n\nexport function bulletList(items: readonly string[], indent = 0): string {\n  const pad = \" \".repeat(indent);\n  return items.map((item) => `${pad}- ${item}`).join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\nexport function heading(level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6, text: string): string {\n  return `${\"#\".repeat(level)} ${text}`;\n}\n\n/** Join non-empty lines, dropping blanks ΓÇö keeps exported files inside the\n *  ~200-line budget without accidental gaps. */\nexport function joinLines(lines: readonly (string | null | undefined)[]): string {\n  return lines.filter((l): l is string => typeof l === \"string\" && l.trim().length > 0).join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Text truncation for UI                                                     */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport function truncate(input: string, max: number): string {\n  if (input.length <= max) return input;\n  const cut = input.slice(0, max - 1);\n  const lastSpace = cut.lastIndexOf(\" \");\n  return `${lastSpace > max * 0.6 ? cut.slice(0, lastSpace) : cut}ΓÇª`;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Monotonic clock                                                            */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * A millisecond clock for measuring durations, safe outside a browser.\n *\n * `performance.now()` where it exists, `Date.now()` otherwise. Duration measurements are used to\n * report how long an embed, a rerank, a guard evaluation or a reasoning run took, and every one\n * of those modules needs the same fallback ΓÇö three private copies would eventually disagree, and\n * a duration measured against two different clocks is a negative number.\n *\n * Monotonic rather than wall-clock on purpose: `Date.now()` can jump backwards if the system\n * clock is corrected mid-run, which would report a negative duration for work that took time.\n */\nexport function monotonicNow(): number {\n  return typeof performance !== \"undefined\" && typeof performance.now === \"function\"\n    ? performance.now()\n    : Date.now();\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 22 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 22 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 22 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\lib\\glob.ts"},"span":[813,831],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Glob matching for guard scoping and `applies_to`.\n *\n * Phase 4 ┬╖ sub-phase 4.3, Phase 5 ┬╖ sub-phase 5.3\n * Plan: plan/phase-4-executable-guards.md ┬º4.3\n *\n * Supports the subset that actually appears in decision records:\n *   *        any run of characters except \"/\"\n *   **       any run of characters including \"/\"\n *   ?        exactly one character except \"/\"\n *   {a,b}    alternation\n *   [abc]    character class\n *\n * Compiled to a RegExp once and cached ΓÇö a guard set is evaluated against every\n * file in the project, so recompiling per file would dominate the runtime.\n */\n\nconst cache = new Map<string, RegExp>();\n\nfunction escapeLiteral(ch: string): string {\n  return /[.+^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/.test(ch) ? `\\\\${ch}` : ch;\n}\n\n/** Translate a glob into a RegExp source string. */\nexport function globToRegExpSource(glob: string): string {\n  let out = \"^\";\n  let i = 0;\n\n  while (i < glob.length) {\n    const ch = glob[i] as string;\n\n    if (ch === \"*\") {\n      if (glob[i + 1] === \"*\") {\n        // `**/` should also match zero directories.\n        if (glob[i + 2] === \"/\") {\n          out += \"(?:.*/)?\";\n          i += 3;\n          continue;\n        }\n        out += \".*\";\n        i += 2;\n        continue;\n      }\n      out += \"[^/]*\";\n      i += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (ch === \"?\") {\n      out += \"[^/]\";\n      i += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (ch === \"{\") {\n      const close = glob.indexOf(\"}\", i);\n      if (close > i) {\n        const options = glob.slice(i + 1, close).split(\",\");\n        out += `(?:${options.map(globToRegExpSource).join(\"|\")})`;\n        i = close + 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n    }\n\n    if (ch === \"[\") {\n      const close = glob.indexOf(\"]\", i);\n      if (close > i) {\n        out += glob.slice(i, close + 1);\n        i = close + 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n    }\n\n    out += escapeLiteral(ch);\n    i += 1;\n  }\n\n  return `${out}$`;\n}\n\nexport function compileGlob(glob: string): RegExp {\n  const cached = cache.get(glob);\n  if (cached) return cached;\n  const re = new RegExp(globToRegExpSource(glob));\n  cache.set(glob, re);\n  return re;\n}\n\nexport function matchesGlob(path: string, glob: string): boolean {\n  return compileGlob(glob).test(path);\n}\n\nexport function matchesAnyGlob(path: string, globs: readonly string[]): boolean {\n  return globs.some((g) => matchesGlob(path, g));\n}\n\nexport function filterByGlob(paths: readonly string[], glob: string): readonly string[] {\n  const re = compileGlob(glob);\n  return paths.filter((p) => re.test(p));\n}\n\n/**\n * Word-boundary pattern matching.\n *\n * Default for every `string_*` guard, because a bare substring match on \"pg\"\n * hits \"pgp\", \"pgsql-tools\" and any passing comment ΓÇö which is how a guard\n * learns to cry wolf. See plan/phase-4 ┬º4.3 and docs/04 ┬º8.\n */\nexport function compilePattern(\n  pattern: string,\n  options: { wordBoundary?: boolean; caseSensitive?: boolean } = {},\n): RegExp {\n  const { wordBoundary = true, caseSensitive = false } = options;\n  const escaped = pattern.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$&\");\n  const source = wordBoundary ? `\\\\b${escaped}\\\\b` : escaped;\n  return new RegExp(source, caseSensitive ? \"g\" : \"gi\");\n}\n\n/** Returns the first matching line and its 1-based number, or null. The evidence\n *  string quoted into a revision proposal comes from here. */\nexport function findMatchInText(\n  text: string,\n  pattern: string,\n  options: { wordBoundary?: boolean; caseSensitive?: boolean } = {},\n): { line: number; text: string } | null {\n  const re = compilePattern(pattern, options);\n  const lines = text.split(\"\\n\");\n  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n    const line = lines[i] ?? \"\";\n    re.lastIndex = 0;\n    if (re.test(line)) return { line: i + 1, text: line.trim() };\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nexport function clearGlobCache(): void {\n  cache.clear();\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Finding globs in prose                                                     */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * A path-shaped string that contains at least one wildcard.\n *\n * Deliberately requires a wildcard: `src/db/schema.ts` is a FILE, and `applies_to`\n * is a scope. Recording a file as a scope would make a decision look narrower than\n * it is, and a guard scoped to one file would pass forever while the decision it\n * protects rotted.\n */\nconst GLOB_IN_TEXT = /(?:^|[\\s`\"'(,])([\\w.@/-]*(?:\\*\\*|\\*|\\?|\\{[\\w,.-]+\\})[\\w.@/*?{},-]*)/g;\n\n/**\n * Every glob mentioned in a stretch of text, de-duplicated, in order of first\n * appearance.\n *\n * Used by Phase 1.3 to fill `appliesTo`, which drives `applies_to` in export\n * (plan/phase-5 ┬º5.3) and guard scoping (plan/phase-4 ┬º4.2).\n */\nexport function findGlobs(text: string, limit = 12): readonly string[] {\n  const found: string[] = [];\n  const seen = new Set<string>();\n\n  for (const match of text.matchAll(GLOB_IN_TEXT)) {\n    const raw = (match[1] ?? \"\").replace(/[,.;:)]+$/g, \"\");\n    if (raw.length === 0) continue;\n\n    const normalised = raw.replace(/^\\/+/, \"\");\n    if (seen.has(normalised)) continue;\n    seen.add(normalised);\n    found.push(normalised);\n\n    if (found.length >= limit) break;\n  }\n\n  return found;\n}\n\n/** Is this string a glob rather than a plain path? */\nexport function isGlob(candidate: string): boolean {\n  return /[*?]|\\{[\\w,.-]+\\}/.test(candidate);\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"suppressions/unused","severity":"warning","description":"Suppression comment has no effect. Remove the suppression or make sure you are suppressing the correct rule.","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Suppression comment has no effect. Remove the suppression or make sure you are suppressing the correct rule."}],"advices":{"advices":[]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\lib\\invariant.ts"},"span":[2202,2272],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Assertion helpers.\n *\n * Used for INTERNAL invariants ΓÇö conditions that must hold if the code is\n * correct. Never for user input, which is validated with `Result` instead.\n *\n * The distinction matters: an invariant violation is a BUG and should throw\n * loudly in development. Bad user input is expected and must degrade gracefully.\n */\n\n/**\n * Throws when a switch or if-chain that was meant to be exhaustive falls\n * through. TypeScript narrows `value` to `never` at the call site, so adding a\n * new union member produces a compile error at every switch that forgot it.\n */\nexport function unreachable(value: never, context?: string): never {\n  const detail = context ? ` (${context})` : \"\";\n  throw new Error(`Unreachable branch reached${detail}: ${String(value)}`);\n}\n\n/** Assert with a readable message. Stripped from production builds by the\n *  bundler when wrapped in `import.meta.env.DEV`. */\nexport function invariant(condition: unknown, message: string): asserts condition {\n  if (!condition) {\n    throw new Error(`Invariant violated: ${message}`);\n  }\n}\n\n/** Non-null assertion that throws rather than silently producing undefined.\n *  Preferred over `!` everywhere ΓÇö `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on, and a\n *  missing map entry is a real bug worth naming. */\nexport function mustExist<T>(value: T | null | undefined, message: string): T {\n  if (value === null || value === undefined) {\n    throw new Error(`Expected a value: ${message}`);\n  }\n  return value;\n}\n\n/** Look up a map entry or throw with the key in the message. */\nexport function mustGet<K, V>(map: ReadonlyMap<K, V>, key: K, label: string): V {\n  const value = map.get(key);\n  if (value === undefined) {\n    throw new Error(`Missing ${label}: ${String(key)}`);\n  }\n  return value;\n}\n\n/**\n * Development-only check. Returns the condition so it can be used inline.\n *\n * `import.meta.env.DEV` is a Vite compile-time constant, so the whole branch is\n * dead-code eliminated from a production build ΓÇö no console access, no `process`\n * reference, and nothing for a bundler to polyfill.\n */\nexport function devAssert(condition: boolean, message: string): boolean {\n  if (!condition && import.meta.env.DEV) {\n    // biome-ignore lint/suspicious/noConsole: development-only diagnostic\n    console.error(`[whylog] ${message}`);\n  }\n  return condition;\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 27 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 27 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 27 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\ontology\\ontology.ts"},"span":[9320,9336],"sourceCode":"/**\n * The ontology facade: one place to ask the vocabulary anything.\n *\n * Phase 0 ┬╖ sub-phase 0.7\n * Plan: plan/phase-0-foundation.md ┬º0.7\n *\n * Every extractor, gate, guard template and exporter reads through this module\n * rather than reaching into `data/`. That is what makes the vocabulary a single\n * artifact: change a table here and every layer above changes with it, with no\n * second copy to keep in sync.\n */\n\nimport type { DecisionVariable, PremiseType } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport { DECISION_VARIABLES, PREMISE_TYPES } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport { ALIASES, GLOBAL_ALIASES, NONE_VALUES } from \"./data/aliases\";\nimport { ANTONYMS, antonymPairCount, antonymsOf, areAntonyms } from \"./data/antonyms\";\nimport {\n  DECISION_CUES,\n  assertiveCues,\n  pivotCues,\n  rejectionCues,\n  speculativeCues,\n} from \"./data/decision-cues\";\nimport {\n  EXCLUSIONS,\n  exclusionCount,\n  exclusionCoverage,\n  exclusionsFor,\n  hasExclusion,\n  primaryExclusion,\n  validateExclusions,\n} from \"./data/exclusions\";\nimport {\n  PREMISE_CUES,\n  cueCountByType,\n  cuesFor,\n  explicitCues,\n  topicalCues,\n} from \"./data/premise-cues\";\nimport {\n  PREMISE_TYPE_META,\n  PREMISE_TYPE_ORDER,\n  allPredicates,\n  isGuardable,\n  isWorldObservable,\n  lookupPredicate,\n  premiseTypesForVariable,\n} from \"./data/premise-types\";\nimport {\n  TEMPLATE_VERIFIABLE_VARIABLES,\n  TYPED_VARIABLES,\n  VARIABLE_META,\n  VARIABLE_ORDER,\n} from \"./data/variables\";\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Re-exports ΓÇö the lookups named in STRUCTURE.md ┬º4                          */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport {\n  canonicalPredicate,\n  canonicalValue,\n  canonicalValueOf,\n  canonicalValueUnscoped,\n  cleanSurface,\n  foldKey,\n  resolveVariable,\n  snakeCase,\n  variableFromContext,\n  variableFromValue,\n  ONTOLOGY_CANONICALIZER,\n} from \"./canonicalize\";\n\nexport { areAntonyms, antonymsOf, antonymPairCount };\nexport { isWorldObservable, isGuardable, lookupPredicate, allPredicates };\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Vocabulary                                                                 */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/** The 14 typed slots plus OTHER. */\nexport const VARIABLES: readonly DecisionVariable[] = DECISION_VARIABLES;\nexport const TYPED_SLOTS: readonly DecisionVariable[] = TYPED_VARIABLES;\nexport const SLOT_ORDER: readonly DecisionVariable[] = VARIABLE_ORDER;\nexport const TEMPLATE_VERIFIABLE: readonly DecisionVariable[] = TEMPLATE_VERIFIABLE_VARIABLES;\n\n/** The closed set of 8. */\nexport const PREMISE_TYPES_CLOSED: readonly PremiseType[] = PREMISE_TYPES;\nexport const PREMISE_ORDER: readonly PremiseType[] = PREMISE_TYPE_ORDER;\n\n/** World-observable = falsifiable by a guard or repo scan. Intent-based = only a\n *  human can retract it. */\nexport const WORLD_OBSERVABLE_TYPES: readonly PremiseType[] =\n  PREMISE_ORDER.filter(isWorldObservable);\nexport const INTENT_BASED_TYPES: readonly PremiseType[] = PREMISE_ORDER.filter(\n  (t) => !isWorldObservable(t),\n);\n\nexport function variableMetaOf(variable: DecisionVariable) {\n  return VARIABLE_META[variable];\n}\n\nexport function premiseMetaOf(type: PremiseType) {\n  return PREMISE_TYPE_META[type];\n}\n\n/** Which premise types to try first for a slot. */\nexport function premiseTypesFor(variable: DecisionVariable): readonly PremiseType[] {\n  return premiseTypesForVariable(VARIABLE_META[variable].typicalPremises);\n}\n\n/** The globs a slot usually governs ΓÇö seeds `applies_to` in export and guard\n *  scoping in Phase 4. */\nexport function typicalGlobsFor(variable: DecisionVariable): readonly string[] {\n  return VARIABLE_META[variable].typicalGlobs;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Cue access                                                                 */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport const DECISION_CUE_LIST = DECISION_CUES;\nexport const PREMISE_CUE_LIST = PREMISE_CUES;\n\nexport { assertiveCues, speculativeCues, rejectionCues, pivotCues };\nexport { cuesFor, explicitCues, topicalCues, cueCountByType };\n\n/** Every cue phrase, sorted longest-first. Longest-first matters: \"do not use\"\n *  must match before \"not\", or the longer, more informative cue is never seen. */\nexport function allCuePhrases(): readonly string[] {\n  const phrases = new Set<string>();\n  for (const cue of DECISION_CUES) phrases.add(cue.phrase);\n  for (const cue of PREMISE_CUES) phrases.add(cue.phrase);\n  return [...phrases].sort((a, b) => b.length - a.length);\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* The structural contradiction check                                         */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface StructuralCheck {\n  readonly contradicts: boolean;\n  readonly why: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * Gate (a) in one call.\n *\n * Two decisions on the same variable whose CANONICAL values are a declared\n * antonym pair. No model, no embedding, no threshold ΓÇö a table lookup. It fires\n * while the semantic gate is still uncertain, and it fires identically every\n * time, which is the property that makes it trustworthy enough to show a user\n * without a confidence bar.\n *\n * The caller must additionally check that both decisions are IN-labelled and\n * that their validity windows OVERLAP: two decisions on the same variable in\n * disjoint intervals are a refinement over time, not a contradiction, and\n * accusing the user of contradicting themselves every time they change their\n * mind sequentially is how a tool gets switched off.\n */\nexport function structuralContradiction(\n  variable: DecisionVariable,\n  valueA: string,\n  valueB: string,\n): StructuralCheck {\n  const result = areAntonyms(variable, valueA, valueB);\n  return result.antonym ? { contradicts: true, why: result.why } : { contradicts: false, why: \"\" };\n}\n\n/** Is this value a deliberate absence? \"no auth at all\" is a DECISION, not a\n *  missing field, and collapsing it into undefined would delete the most\n *  interesting kind of choice. */\nexport function isNoneValue(value: string): boolean {\n  return NONE_VALUES.has(value.trim().toLowerCase());\n}\n\n/** All values that would contradict `value` under `variable`. Used to pre-empt a\n * decision while it is being authored rather than detecting it afterwards. */\nexport function contradictsValue(variable: DecisionVariable, value: string): readonly string[] {\n  return antonymsOf(variable, value);\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Self-description                                                           */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport interface OntologySize {\n  readonly variables: number;\n  readonly typedVariables: number;\n  readonly premiseTypes: number;\n  readonly predicates: number;\n  readonly aliases: number;\n  readonly antonymPairs: number;\n  readonly decisionCues: number;\n  readonly premiseCues: number;\n  readonly worldObservableTypes: number;\n  /** Curated OUT-list rules. Zero would mean every excluded condition is emitted\n   *  as a mechanical negation, which is correct logic and unreadable English. */\n  readonly exclusions: number;\n}\n\n/** Counts, for the health panel and for the pitch. Every number here is derived\n *  from the tables, so the panel cannot drift from the data. */\nexport function ontologySize(): OntologySize {\n  let aliases = Object.keys(GLOBAL_ALIASES).length;\n  for (const variable of Object.keys(ALIASES) as DecisionVariable[]) {\n    aliases += Object.keys(ALIASES[variable]).length;\n  }\n\n  return {\n    variables: DECISION_VARIABLES.length,\n    typedVariables: TYPED_VARIABLES.length,\n    premiseTypes: PREMISE_TYPES.length,\n    predicates: allPredicates().length,\n    aliases,\n    antonymPairs: antonymPairCount(),\n    decisionCues: DECISION_CUES.length,\n    premiseCues: PREMISE_CUES.length,\n    worldObservableTypes: WORLD_OBSERVABLE_TYPES.length,\n    exclusions: exclusionCount(),\n  };\n}\n\n/** One paragraph describing the vocabulary, for the About panel and for the\n *  exported README. Written from the tables so it never goes stale. */\nexport function describeOntology(): string {\n  const size = ontologySize();\n  const observable = WORLD_OBSERVABLE_TYPES.join(\", \");\n  const intent = INTENT_BASED_TYPES.join(\", \");\n  return [\n    `${size.typedVariables} typed decision slots (plus OTHER as a tracked escape hatch), `,\n    `${size.premiseTypes} premise types with ${size.predicates} standard predicates, `,\n    `${size.aliases} value aliases, ${size.antonymPairs} variable-scoped antonym pairs ` +\n      `and ${size.exclusions} exclusion rules over ${exclusionCoverage()} predicates. `,\n    `World-observable premise types ΓÇö falsifiable by a guard: ${observable}. `,\n    `Intent-based ΓÇö retractable only by a human: ${intent}.`,\n  ].join(\"\");\n}\n\n/** Sanity check the tables against each other. Returns human-readable problems;\n *  an empty array means the ontology is internally consistent. Run at boot in\n *  development so a broken table is never shipped. */\nexport function validateOntology(): readonly string[] {\n  const problems: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const variable of DECISION_VARIABLES) {\n    if (!VARIABLE_META[variable]) problems.push(`${variable}: no entry in VARIABLE_META`);\n  }\n\n  for (const type of PREMISE_TYPES) {\n    const meta = PREMISE_TYPE_META[type];\n    if (!meta) {\n      problems.push(`${type}: no entry in PREMISE_TYPE_META`);\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (meta.namespace !== type) {\n      problems.push(`${type}: namespace \"${meta.namespace}\" does not match the type name`);\n    }\n    for (const predicate of meta.predicates) {\n      if (lookupPredicate(`${type}.${predicate.name}`) === null) {\n        problems.push(`${type}.${predicate.name}: not resolvable by lookupPredicate`);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Antonyms must be over CANONICAL values: if a pair names a surface form, gate\n  // (a) silently never fires for it.\n  for (const variable of DECISION_VARIABLES) {\n    for (const pair of ANTONYMS[variable]) {\n      for (const value of [pair.a, pair.b]) {\n        const isAlias = Object.keys(ALIASES[variable]).includes(value);\n        if (isAlias) {\n          problems.push(\n            `${variable}: antonym pair uses the ALIAS \"${value}\", not its canonical form ΓÇö gate (a) would never fire on it`,\n          );\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (const cue of PREMISE_CUES) {\n    if (cue.predicate !== undefined && lookupPredicate(cue.predicate) === null) {\n      problems.push(`premise cue \"${cue.phrase}\": predicate ${cue.predicate} is not in the table`);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // The OUT-list vocabulary must resolve too: an exclusion naming a predicate that\n  // does not exist produces a node the identity ladder cannot pool, which means a\n  // fresh duplicate on every import.\n  problems.push(...validateExclusions());\n\n  return problems;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Exclusions ΓÇö the OUT-list vocabulary (Phase 1.4)                            */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport { exclusionsFor, primaryExclusion, hasExclusion, exclusionCount, exclusionCoverage };\nexport const EXCLUSION_RULES = EXCLUSIONS;\nexport type { ExclusionRule } from \"./data/exclusions\";\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 24 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 24 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 24 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\reasoning\\cascade.ts"},"span":[4191,4194],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Cascade ΓÇö 2.7.\n *\n * Phase 2 ┬╖ sub-phase 2.7\n * Plan: plan/phase-2-truth-maintenance.md ┬º2.7\n * Docs: docs/02-truth-maintenance-theory.md ┬º3\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * ONE PREMISE FALLS. WHAT ELSE BECOMES UNSOUND?\n *\n * Not \"everything downstream\". That is the naive answer and it is the one that gets a\n * tool switched off.\n *\n * A decision whose justification loses an IN-list premise becomes unsound ONLY IF that\n * justification was its only support. A decision with two independent IN justifications\n * keeps one, and stays IN. Propagation STOPS there.\n *\n * This is the difference between a truth-maintenance system and a notification spam\n * cannon, and it is why risk R2 (\"the cascade cries wolf\") is the second-largest risk\n * in the plan. Over-retraction is worse than under-retraction: a missed dependency is\n * recoverable on the next run, but a user who is told that eleven of their decisions\n * are unsound when one premise changed will stop reading the list.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE STOP CONDITION IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT AN OPTIMISATION\n *\n * `stoppedAtIndependentSupport: true` is recorded on the step where propagation ended\n * because the node had surviving support. It is surfaced in the UI as a deliberate\n * non-event:\n *\n *   \"WLD-0004 also rests on `single-user`, but it has a second independent\n *    justification that still holds. It stays IN. Propagation stopped here.\"\n *\n * Showing WHY something was not retracted is as important as showing what was. Without\n * it the user cannot tell \"we checked and it is fine\" from \"we did not look\".\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * DEPTH CAP\n *\n * Six levels. Beyond that the chain is no longer something a human can follow in one\n * reading, and `truncated` plus `potentiallyAffectedBeyondCap` report that there is\n * more rather than silently pretending the walk finished.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { GraphState } from \"@/substrate/graph-state\";\nimport type { DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { DecisionLabel } from \"@/types/domain\";\nimport type { CascadeResult, CascadeStep } from \"@/types/reasoning\";\nimport {\n  type ReasoningIndex,\n  decisionsRestingOn,\n  indexGraph,\n  justificationsFor,\n  premisesNamedBy,\n} from \"./graph-index\";\nimport type { Labelled, NodeId } from \"./labeller\";\n\n/** Fallback when no cap is supplied. The store passes `settings.cascadeDepthCap`. */\nexport const MAX_CASCADE_DEPTH = 4;\n\n/**\n * Propagate one retraction.\n *\n * @param retractedPremises the premises that just became false. Propagation starts\n *   from every justification that named one of them.\n */\nexport function cascade(\n  state: GraphState,\n  labels: ReadonlyMap<NodeId, Labelled>,\n  retractedPremises: readonly PremiseId[],\n  depthCap: number = MAX_CASCADE_DEPTH,\n): CascadeResult {\n  /* A cap of 0 would mean \"do not propagate at all\", which is a legitimate setting ΓÇö\n   * but a NEGATIVE cap would make every step truncated and report the whole graph as\n   * unexamined. Clamped, not trusted. */\n  const cap = Math.max(0, Math.floor(Number.isFinite(depthCap) ? depthCap : MAX_CASCADE_DEPTH));\n  const steps: CascadeStep[] = [];\n  const flipped: DecisionId[] = [];\n  const survivors: DecisionId[] = [];\n  const visited = new Set<NodeId>();\n  let truncated = false;\n  let beyondCap = 0;\n\n  const retractedSet = new Set<PremiseId>(retractedPremises);\n  const index = indexGraph(state);\n\n  /* Seed: every decision resting on a retracted premise, via the shared-premise index.\n   * This is the ONLY edge the cascade follows ΓÇö never `related`, never topical\n   * similarity. ┬º2.7: \"Cascade follows shared-premise edges only.\" */\n  const seeds: DecisionId[] = [];\n  for (const premiseId of retractedSet) {\n    for (const decisionId of decisionsRestingOn(index, premiseId)) {\n      if (!seeds.includes(decisionId)) seeds.push(decisionId);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const propagate = (decisionId: DecisionId, from: DecisionId | PremiseId, depth: number): void => {\n    if (visited.has(decisionId)) return;\n    visited.add(decisionId);\n\n    if (depth > cap) {\n      truncated = true;\n      beyondCap += 1;\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const decision = state.decisions.get(decisionId);\n    if (decision === undefined) return;\n\n    const current: DecisionLabel = labels.get(decisionId)?.label ?? decision.label;\n\n    /* ---- THE STOP CONDITION ---- */\n    if (hasIndependentSupport(index, labels, decisionId, retractedSet)) {\n      steps.push({\n        from,\n        to: decisionId,\n        depth,\n        became: current,\n        stoppedAtIndependentSupport: true,\n        reason:\n          \"Another justification for this decision does not depend on any retracted premise. It still holds, so the decision stays as it is. Propagation stops here.\",\n      });\n      survivors.push(decisionId);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const became: DecisionLabel = current === \"IN\" ? \"OUT\" : current;\n    steps.push({\n      from,\n      to: decisionId,\n      depth,\n      became,\n      stoppedAtIndependentSupport: false,\n      reason:\n        became === \"OUT\"\n          ? \"Every justification for this decision named a premise that is no longer true. Without them it has no support.\"\n          : `Already ${current}; no support was lost.`,\n    });\n\n    if (became !== current) flipped.push(decisionId);\n\n    /* ---- the second hop ----\n     * This decision was contributing support to the premises it names. Anything ELSE\n     * resting on those premises is next in line ΓÇö but only if this decision was their\n     * last support, which `hasIndependentSupport` decides at the next level. Following\n     * the shared premise is following a shared-premise edge; following `dependsOn` or\n     * `related` here would be exactly the over-retraction ┬º2.7 forbids. */\n    for (const premiseId of premisesNamedBy(index, decisionId)) {\n      for (const dependent of decisionsRestingOn(index, premiseId)) {\n        if (visited.has(dependent)) continue;\n        propagate(dependent, premiseId, depth + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  };\n\n  for (const seed of seeds) {\n    /* `from` names the NODE the propagation came through. The first retracted premise\n     * this seed rests on is the honest answer; a justification id is not a node. */\n    const via = firstRetractedPremiseOf(index, seed, retractedSet);\n    propagate(seed, via ?? seed, 0);\n  }\n\n  return { steps, flipped, survivors, truncated, potentiallyAffectedBeyondCap: beyondCap };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* The stop condition                                                         */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * Does this decision have a justification that survives the retraction?\n *\n * A justification survives when every one of its IN-list premises is still supported\n * and none of its OUT-list premises has become true. That is exactly the\n * well-foundedness condition from `labeller.ts`, evaluated against the post-retraction\n * world rather than the current one.\n */\nfunction hasIndependentSupport(\n  index: ReasoningIndex,\n  labels: ReadonlyMap<NodeId, Labelled>,\n  decisionId: DecisionId,\n  retracted: ReadonlySet<PremiseId>,\n): boolean {\n  for (const justification of justificationsFor(index, decisionId)) {\n    const namedRetracted = justification.inList.some((premiseId) => retracted.has(premiseId));\n    if (namedRetracted) continue;\n\n    const inListHolds = justification.inList.every((premiseId) => {\n      if (retracted.has(premiseId)) return false;\n      const labelled = labels.get(premiseId);\n      /* An unlabelled premise is an assumption: no justification, presumed true. */\n      return labelled === undefined ? true : labelled.label === \"IN\";\n    });\n    if (!inListHolds) continue;\n\n    const outListClear = justification.outList.every((premiseId) => {\n      const labelled = labels.get(premiseId);\n      return labelled === undefined ? true : labelled.label !== \"IN\";\n    });\n    if (!outListClear) continue;\n\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  return false;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Graph access                                                               */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nfunction firstRetractedPremiseOf(\n  index: ReasoningIndex,\n  decisionId: DecisionId,\n  retracted: ReadonlySet<PremiseId>,\n): PremiseId | null {\n  for (const justification of justificationsFor(index, decisionId)) {\n    for (const premiseId of justification.inList) {\n      if (retracted.has(premiseId)) return premiseId;\n    }\n    for (const premiseId of justification.outList) {\n      if (retracted.has(premiseId)) return premiseId;\n    }\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Reporting                                                                  */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * The cascade as a sentence a user can act on.\n *\n * Leads with the count that matters ΓÇö decisions that changed ΓÇö and reports stops and\n * truncation explicitly, because \"we checked four and three were fine\" is a different\n * message from \"we checked one\".\n */\nexport function describeCascade(_state: GraphState, result: CascadeResult): string {\n  if (result.steps.length === 0) {\n    return \"Nothing else depended on it. No other decision changed.\";\n  }\n\n  const parts: string[] = [];\n\n  if (result.flipped.length > 0) {\n    parts.push(\n      `${result.flipped.length} decision${result.flipped.length === 1 ? \"\" : \"s\"} became unsound: ${result.flipped.join(\", \")}.`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (result.survivors.length > 0) {\n    parts.push(\n      `${result.survivors.length} had independent support and ${result.survivors.length === 1 ? \"stays\" : \"stay\"} as ${result.survivors.length === 1 ? \"it is\" : \"they are\"}: ${result.survivors.join(\", \")}.`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (result.truncated) {\n    parts.push(\n      `Propagation stopped at the depth cap with ${result.potentiallyAffectedBeyondCap} decision(s) not examined. There may be more downstream than this shows.`,\n    );\n  }\n\n  return parts.join(\" \");\n}\n\n/** The ordered walk, for the cascade view: `from ΓåÆ to (depth d) ΓÇö reason`. */\nexport function cascadeLines(state: GraphState, result: CascadeResult): readonly string[] {\n  return result.steps.map((step) => {\n    const fromName = nameOf(state, step.from);\n    const toName = nameOf(state, step.to);\n    const marker = step.stoppedAtIndependentSupport ? \"STOPS\" : `becomes ${step.became}`;\n    return `${\"  \".repeat(step.depth)}${fromName} ΓåÆ ${toName} ΓÇö ${marker}. ${step.reason}`;\n  });\n}\n\nfunction nameOf(state: GraphState, nodeId: DecisionId | PremiseId): string {\n  const decision = state.decisions.get(nodeId as DecisionId);\n  if (decision !== undefined) return `${decision.id} \"${decision.value}\"`;\n\n  const premise = state.premises.get(nodeId as PremiseId);\n  if (premise !== undefined) return `${premise.id} \"${premise.text}\"`;\n\n  return nodeId;\n}\n\n/** Depth actually reached ΓÇö used by Phase 7 to report whether the cap ever binds. */\nexport function deepestStep(result: CascadeResult): number {\n  return result.steps.reduce((max, step) => (step.depth > max ? step.depth : max), 0);\n}\n\n/** The cap in force, for the health panel ΓÇö a truncated cascade is only interpretable\n *  next to the number that truncated it. */\nexport function describeCap(depthCap: number = MAX_CASCADE_DEPTH): string {\n  return `cascade depth cap ${depthCap} (shared-premise edges only, never topical similarity)`;\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 33 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 33 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 33 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\reasoning\\backtracking.ts"},"span":[4588,4591],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Dependency-directed backtracking ΓÇö 2.6.\n *\n * Phase 2 ┬╖ sub-phase 2.6\n * Plan: plan/phase-2-truth-maintenance.md ┬º2.6\n * Docs: docs/02-truth-maintenance-theory.md ┬º3\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY THIS IS NOT A GRAPH SEARCH\n *\n * The naive move is to walk the justification graph from the failed decision and\n * report everything reachable. That answers \"what depends on this?\" ΓÇö which the\n * cascade in `cascade.ts` already does ΓÇö and not the question backtracking exists to\n * answer: **which assumptions, minimally, are responsible for this belief being\n * unsound?**\n *\n * The distinction is visible in the demo fixture.\n *\n *   WLD-0001 \"SQLite, not Postgres\" is justified by four IN-list premises.\n *   New information arrives: two users.\n *\n * Reachability says: \"all four premises, plus the decision, plus everything below\".\n * Backtracking says: \"ONE premise ΓÇö `single-user` ΓÇö is the culprit. The other three\n * still hold. Give up that one and nothing else.\"\n *\n * Minimal retraction is the entire point of dependency-directed backtracking, and it\n * is what makes the proposal actionable. \"Everything you believed is now suspect\" is\n * not a proposal.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THIS GRAPH IS BIPARTITE, WHICH CHANGES WHAT \"BACKWARDS\" MEANS\n *\n * `Justification.consequent` is a `DecisionId` and its lists are `PremiseId`s, so a\n * premise is never a consequent: **every premise is an assumption.** Doyle's\n * \"antecedents of antecedents, stopping at nodes with no incoming arcs\" reaches bedrock\n * in ONE hop here, because there is no derived-premise layer to walk through.\n *\n * So the culprit set is not found by depth. It is found by asking which premises\n * actually broke the justification:\n *\n *   ┬╖ an IN-list premise that is no longer IN   ΓÇö the support was withdrawn\n *   ┬╖ an OUT-list premise that is now IN        ΓÇö an excluded condition arrived\n *\n * That set is minimal BY CONSTRUCTION, which is what ┬º2.6's ATMS borrowing asks for.\n * Premises that still hold are examined and cleared, and the explanation says so ΓÇö\n * \"the other three still hold\" is the half of the diagnosis that makes it actionable.\n *\n * Depth does exist in this graph, but it comes from `Decision.edges.dependsOn`, not from\n * the justification arcs. That is what the walk follows when it needs to go deeper than\n * one hop, and it is the only way a failure reaches an assumption belonging to a\n * DIFFERENT decision.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THE TRUNCATION FLAG IS NOT COSMETIC\n *\n * Depth is capped because a cyclic justification set can loop forever (a cycle cannot\n * be well-founded, but a log can still CONTAIN one if events arrive in an order that\n * defeats the check). `truncated: true` must reach the user: a backtracking result\n * that stopped early is a different answer from one that finished, and presenting\n * them identically would make an incomplete diagnosis look complete.\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n */\n\nimport type { GraphState } from \"@/substrate/graph-state\";\nimport type { DecisionId, PremiseId } from \"@/types/common\";\nimport type { BacktrackResult } from \"@/types/reasoning\";\nimport { indexGraph, justificationsFor } from \"./graph-index\";\nimport type { Labelled, NodeId } from \"./labeller\";\n\n/** Deeper than this and the graph is almost certainly cyclic or the diagnosis has\n *  stopped being useful to a human. */\nexport const MAX_BACKTRACK_DEPTH = 6;\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Entry point                                                                */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * Find the minimal set of assumptions responsible for `decisionId` being unsound.\n *\n * Returns premises, not decisions. A decision is a conclusion; you do not fix a\n * broken conclusion by removing it, you fix it by removing the assumption that no\n * longer holds and letting the label propagate.\n */\nexport function backtrack(\n  state: GraphState,\n  labels: ReadonlyMap<NodeId, Labelled>,\n  decisionId: DecisionId,\n): BacktrackResult {\n  const index = indexGraph(state);\n  const path: (DecisionId | PremiseId)[] = [];\n  const culprits = new Set<PremiseId>();\n  const visited = new Set<DecisionId>();\n  let deepest = 0;\n  let truncated = false;\n\n  const walk = (nodeId: DecisionId, depth: number): void => {\n    if (visited.has(nodeId)) return;\n    visited.add(nodeId);\n\n    if (depth > deepest) deepest = depth;\n    if (depth > MAX_BACKTRACK_DEPTH) {\n      truncated = true;\n      return;\n    }\n\n    path.push(nodeId);\n\n    /* ---- the culprit test: which premises actually broke the justification ---- */\n    for (const justification of justificationsFor(index, nodeId)) {\n      for (const premiseId of justification.inList) {\n        const label = labels.get(premiseId)?.label;\n        /* An unlabelled premise is an assumption presumed true. Only a premise that\n         * has been actively labelled something other than IN withdrew its support. */\n        if (label !== undefined && label !== \"IN\") {\n          culprits.add(premiseId);\n          path.push(premiseId);\n        }\n      }\n\n      /* The non-monotonic half, and the one gate (c) exists for: an EXCLUDED condition\n       * that arrived. This premise being IN is what invalidates the justification. */\n      for (const premiseId of justification.outList) {\n        if (labels.get(premiseId)?.label === \"IN\") {\n          culprits.add(premiseId);\n          path.push(premiseId);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    /* ---- depth: `dependsOn`, the only arc that goes deeper in a bipartite graph ---- */\n    const decision = state.decisions.get(nodeId);\n    if (decision === undefined) return;\n\n    for (const dependency of decision.edges.dependsOn) {\n      if (visited.has(dependency)) continue;\n      /* Only follow a dependency that is itself in trouble. Walking a sound dependency\n       * would import its premises into the culprit set and inflate it, which is the one\n       * thing minimality forbids. */\n      if (labels.get(dependency)?.label === \"IN\") continue;\n      walk(dependency, depth + 1);\n    }\n  };\n\n  walk(decisionId, 0);\n\n  return {\n    culpritPremises: [...culprits],\n    path,\n    depthReached: deepest,\n    truncated,\n  };\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* The premises a single decision rests on                                    */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/**\n * Every premise in any supporting IN-list of a decision, with OUT-list premises kept\n * separate.\n *\n * This is the conjunction that goes into a nogood: \"these cannot all hold\". It is\n * deliberately NOT filtered to the culprits ΓÇö a nogood records the whole set that was\n * believed together, because that is what cannot be re-asserted together.\n */\nexport function assumptionSetOf(\n  state: GraphState,\n  labels: ReadonlyMap<NodeId, Labelled>,\n  decisionId: DecisionId,\n): { readonly inList: readonly PremiseId[]; readonly outList: readonly PremiseId[] } {\n  const index = indexGraph(state);\n  const inList = new Set<PremiseId>();\n  const outList = new Set<PremiseId>();\n\n  for (const justification of justificationsFor(index, decisionId)) {\n    /* Only the justifications that were HOLDING the decision up. Including an already\n     * invalid one would put premises into the nogood that the decision never actually\n     * relied on, and a nogood that overstates the conjunction blocks re-assertions it\n     * has no business blocking. */\n    if (labels.get(justification.id)?.label !== \"IN\") continue;\n    for (const premiseId of justification.inList) inList.add(premiseId);\n    for (const premiseId of justification.outList) outList.add(premiseId);\n  }\n\n  /* A decision whose every justification has already failed has no IN-labelled one to\n   * read, and its nogood would come back empty. Fall back to all of them: the\n   * conjunction that WAS believed is still the conjunction that cannot hold. */\n  if (inList.size === 0 && outList.size === 0) {\n    for (const justification of justificationsFor(index, decisionId)) {\n      for (const premiseId of justification.inList) inList.add(premiseId);\n      for (const premiseId of justification.outList) outList.add(premiseId);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return { inList: [...inList], outList: [...outList] };\n}\n\n/**\n * The derived premises the walk could not get underneath.\n *\n * In a bipartite graph this is only ever non-empty when the DEPTH CAP bound a\n * `dependsOn` chain ΓÇö there is no derived-premise layer to fail to reach. Kept because\n * ┬º2.6 requires a partial result to be labelled partial rather than presented as\n * complete, and the cap is the one way this walk can come up short.\n */\nexport function partialCulprits(\n  state: GraphState,\n  labels: ReadonlyMap<NodeId, Labelled>,\n  decisionId: DecisionId,\n): readonly PremiseId[] {\n  const result = backtrack(state, labels, decisionId);\n  if (!result.truncated) return [];\n\n  const culprits = new Set(result.culpritPremises);\n  return result.path.filter((nodeId): nodeId is PremiseId => {\n    const asPremise = nodeId as PremiseId;\n    return state.premises.has(asPremise) && !culprits.has(asPremise);\n  });\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n/* Reporting                                                                  */\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nexport function describeBacktrack(state: GraphState, result: BacktrackResult): string {\n  if (result.culpritPremises.length === 0) {\n    return \"No culprit found ΓÇö the decision is supported by assumptions that still hold.\";\n  }\n\n  const named = result.culpritPremises\n    .map((premiseId) => `\"${state.premises.get(premiseId)?.text ?? premiseId}\"`)\n    .join(\", \");\n\n  /* Minimality is stated explicitly: it is the difference between a diagnosis and a\n   * list, and the user has to be told that the other premises were examined and cleared\n   * rather than never looked at. */\n\n  const singular = result.culpritPremises.length === 1;\n\n  return [\n    `${singular ? \"One assumption is\" : `${result.culpritPremises.length} assumptions are`} responsible: ${named}.`,\n    singular\n      ? \"Retracting it alone is enough. Every other premise this decision rests on was examined and still holds.\"\n      : \"This is the smallest set that resolves it ΓÇö give up as little of it as the new evidence requires. The other premises were examined and still hold.\",\n    `Searched to depth ${result.depthReached}.`,\n    result.truncated\n      ? `TRUNCATED at depth ${MAX_BACKTRACK_DEPTH} ΓÇö there may be culprits deeper than this result shows.`\n      : \"\",\n  ]\n    .filter((part) => part.length > 0)\n    .join(\" \");\n}\n\n/**\n * The assumptions a human would recognise as assumptions.\n *\n * `requirement` and `team` premises express intent rather than a state of the world;\n * blaming them for an unsound decision reads as nonsense (\"your belief failed because\n * you wanted offline mode\"). The diagnosis leads with the world-observable ones and\n * lists the intent-based ones separately, marked as human-only.\n */\nexport function splitCulprits(\n  state: GraphState,\n  culprits: readonly PremiseId[],\n): { readonly worldObservable: readonly PremiseId[]; readonly intentBased: readonly PremiseId[] } {\n  const worldObservable: PremiseId[] = [];\n  const intentBased: PremiseId[] = [];\n\n  for (const premiseId of culprits) {\n    const premise = state.premises.get(premiseId);\n    if (premise === undefined) continue;\n    if (premise.type === \"requirement\" || premise.type === \"team\") intentBased.push(premiseId);\n    else worldObservable.push(premiseId);\n  }\n\n  return { worldObservable, intentBased };\n}\n"},"tags":[],"source":null},{"category":"lint/complexity/noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity","severity":"warning","description":"Excessive complexity of 17 detected (max: 15).","message":[{"elements":[],"content":"Excessive complexity of 17 detected (max: 15)."}],"advices":{"advices":[{"log":["info",[{"elements":[],"content":"Please refactor this function to reduce its complexity score from 17 to the max allowed complexity 15."}]]}]},"verboseAdvices":{"advices":[]},"location":{"path":{"file":".\\src\\reasoning\\backtracking.ts"},"span":[7106,7121],"sourceCode":"/**\n * Dependency-directed backtracking ΓÇö 2.6.\n *\n * Phase 2 ┬╖ sub-phase 2.6\n * Plan: plan/phase-2-truth-maintenance.md ┬º2.6\n * Docs: docs/02-truth-maintenance-theory.md ┬º3\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY THIS IS NOT A GRAPH SEARCH\n *\n * The naive move is to walk the justification graph from the failed decision and\n * report everything reachable. That answers \"what depends on this?\" ΓÇö which the\n * cascade in `cascade.ts` already does ΓÇö and not the question backtracking exists to\n * answer: **which assumptions, minimally, are responsible for this belief being\n * unsound?**\n *\n * The distinction is visible in the demo fixture.\n *\n *   WLD-0001 \"SQLite, not Postgres\" is justified by four IN-list premises.\n *   New information arrives: two users.\n *\n * Reachability says: \"all four premises, plus the decision, plus everything below\".\n * Backtracking says: \"ONE premise ΓÇö `single-user` ΓÇö is the culprit. The other three\n * still hold. Give up that one and nothing else.\"\n *\n * Minimal retraction is the entire point of dependency-directed backtracking, and it\n * is what makes the proposal actionable. \"Everything you believed is now suspect\" is\n * not a proposal.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * THIS GRAPH IS BIPARTITE, WHICH CHANGES WHAT \"BACKWARDS\" MEANS\n *\n * `Justification.consequent` is a `DecisionId` and its lists are `PremiseId`s, so a\n * premise is never a consequent: **every premise is an assumption.** Doyle's\n * \"antecedents of antecedents, stopping at nodes with no incoming arcs\" reaches bedrock\n * in ONE hop here, because there is no derived-premise layer to walk through.\n *\n * So the culprit set is not found by depth. It is found by asking which premises\n * actually broke the justification:\n *\n *   ┬╖ an IN-list premise that is no longer IN   ΓÇö the support was withdrawn\n *   ┬╖ an OUT-list premise that is now IN        ΓÇö an excluded condition arrived\n *\n * That set is minimal BY CONSTRUCTION, which is what ┬º2.6's ATMS borrowing asks for.\n * Premises that still hold are examined and cleared, and the explanation says so ΓÇö\n * \"the other three still hold\" is the half of the diagnosis that makes it actionable.\n *\n * Depth does exist in this graph, but it comes from `Decision.edges.dependsOn`, not from\n * the justification arcs. That is what the walk follows when it needs to go deeper than\n * one hop, and it is the only way a failure reaches an assumption belonging to a\n * DIFFERENT decision.\n *\n * -